OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026", "title": "Exact Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer Formula from Low Selmer Corank", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/exact-bsd-low-selmer-corank.pdf", "family": "002", "family_title": "The full BSD formula from low Selmer corank", "abstract_html": "We prove the full Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over ℚ whose full <i>q</i>-power Selmer group has corank zero or one at some prime <i>q</i>. The analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks equal that corank, and the Tate–Shafarevich group is finite. The formula includes all prime factors and requires no additional hypotheses on reduction, rational torsion, isogenies, complex multiplication, or residual Galois representations.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) be an elliptic curve and \\(q\\) a prime.</p><p class=\"li\">The full \\(p\\)-power Selmer group uses the local Kummer images at every place. Put\n  \\[\n  s_p(E)=\\operatorname{corank}_{\\mathbb Z_p}\\operatorname{Sel}_{p^\\infty}(E/\\mathbb Q),\n  \\qquad\n  a(E)=\\operatorname{ord}_{s=1}L(E,s),\n  \\qquad\n  r(E)=\\operatorname{rank}E(\\mathbb Q).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The function \\(L(E,s)\\) is the uncompleted Hasse–Weil \\(L\\)-function, including every finite Euler factor.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)=\n  \\ker\\!\\left(H^1(\\mathbb Q,E)\\longrightarrow\\prod_vH^1(\\mathbb Q_v,E)\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For the Néron differential \\(\\omega_E\\) of a global minimal equation, define\n  \\[\n  \\Omega_E=\\int_{E(\\mathbb R)}|\\omega_E|,\n  \\qquad\n  c_\\ell(E)=[E(\\mathbb Q_\\ell):E^0(\\mathbb Q_\\ell)].\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\Omega_E\\) includes both real components when there are two.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x(P)=a/b\\) in lowest terms with \\(b&gt;0\\), set \\(h_x(P)=\\log\\max\\{|a|,b\\}\\), \\(h_x(O)=0\\), and\n  \\[\n  H(P)=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}4^{-n}h_x([2^n]P),\n  \\qquad\n  B(P,Q)=\\frac{H(P+Q)-H(P)-H(Q)}2.\n  \\]\n  For a basis \\(P_1,\\ldots,P_r\\) of \\(E(\\mathbb Q)/E(\\mathbb Q)_{\\mathrm{tors}}\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Reg}_E=\\det(B(P_i,P_j));\n  \\]\n  in rank zero set \\(\\operatorname{Reg}_E=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(s_q(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nr(E)=a(E)=s_q(E),\\qquad \\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nand, with \\(r=r(E)\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{L^{(r)}(E,1)}{r!}\n=\n\\frac{\\Omega_E\\operatorname{Reg}_E\\,\n\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)\\prod_{\\ell\\ \\mathrm{finite}}c_\\ell(E)}\n{\\#E(\\mathbb Q)_{\\mathrm{tors}}^2}.\n\\]\nThere are no additional hypotheses on reduction, rational torsion, isogenies, complex multiplication, or residual Galois representations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 1’s unrestricted low-corank input at the prime \\(q\\). It gives\n\\[\na(E)=r(E)=s_q(E)\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThe Kummer exact sequence then gives \\(s_p(E)=r(E)\\) for every \\(p\\), in particular \\(s_2(E)\\leq1\\).</p><p>Proposition 1’s two-primary input makes \\(Q_E\\) positive rational and gives its correct valuation at two. Proposition 21 gives the correct valuation at every odd prime, independently of \\(q\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{Q_E}{\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)}\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}\n\\]\nhas zero valuation at every rational prime, hence equals one. Substituting the definition of \\(Q_E\\) is exactly the asserted leading-term identity with the total real period and full Mordell–Weil regulator.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Arithmetic reductions and compatible auxiliary fields.", "statement": "<p>Arithmetic reductions and compatible auxiliary fields.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the periods, heights, regulator, and full Selmer groups of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For an elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\) of analytic rank \\(r\\leq1\\), define\n  \\[\n  Q_A=\n  \\frac{L^{(r)}(A,1)\\#A(\\mathbb Q)_{\\mathrm{tors}}^2}\n  {r!\\Omega_A\\operatorname{Reg}_A\\prod_\\ell c_\\ell(A)},\n  \\qquad\n  X_p(A)=v_p(Q_A)-v_p(\\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)),\n  \\]\n  where \\(v_p(p)=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The low-corank hypothesis in Theorem 1 implies rank equality, finiteness of the entire Tate–Shafarevich group, and \\(s_p(E)=r(E)\\) for every prime \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every curve of analytic rank at most one, \\(Q_A\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}\\), \\(X_p(A)\\) is defined, and \\(X_2(A)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The ratio \\(Q_A/\\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)\\) is invariant under rational isogeny.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For each fixed odd prime \\(p\\) and each \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) of analytic rank \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), there are auxiliary fields \\(K,F,L\\) and a companion \\(E'=E^{D'}\\) satisfying Lemma 1.6 below. In particular, both \\(E/K\\) and \\(E'/K\\) have rank one and finite Tate–Shafarevich group.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Kummer exact sequence is\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow A(\\mathbb Q)\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p/\\mathbb Z_p\n\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Sel}_{p^\\infty}(A/\\mathbb Q)\n\\longrightarrow\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)[p^\\infty]\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nAfter Lemma 1.1, finiteness of the entire Tate–Shafarevich group gives \\(s_p(E)=r(E)\\) for every \\(p\\), so Lemma 1.2 applies. For any auxiliary curve of analytic rank at most one, Lemma 1.3 and the same sequence permit application of Lemma 1.2. This proves the rank, finiteness, rationality, positivity, and two-primary assertions.</p><p>Lemma 1.4 proves isogeny invariance. Lemma 1.6 and the quadratic restriction-of-scalars isogenies give rank one and finite Tate–Shafarevich group over \\(K\\) for \\(E\\) and \\(E'=E^{D'}\\). A nonzero point in either rank-one line has nonzero local logarithm at both places above \\(p\\), because the local elliptic logarithm has torsion kernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1, “Unrestricted low-corank converse.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\), every prime \\(q\\), and \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), \\( s_q(A)=u \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a(A)=r(A)=u,\\qquad \\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)<\\infty. \\)", "statement": "<p>For every elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\), every prime \\(q\\), and \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}\\),\n\\[\ns_q(A)=u\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na(A)=r(A)=u,\\qquad \\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the external input [23, Theorem 1.1]. The paper uses its stated rank and finiteness conclusion and does not interpret its rationalized cohomological construction as an integral leading-term formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Theorem 2.2, “Two-primary leading term.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(s_2(A)\\leq1\\), the quotient \\(Q_A\\), formed with the total real period and the full-lattice regulator above, is a positive rational number and …", "statement": "<p>For every elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(s_2(A)\\leq1\\), the quotient \\(Q_A\\), formed with the total real period and the full-lattice regulator above, is a positive rational number and\n\\[\nv_2(Q_A)=v_2(\\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the external input [24, Theorem 1.1]. Its role is the assertion at two, together with positivity and rationality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Theorem 2.3, “Analytic rank zero and one.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If an elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\) satisfies \\(a(A)\\leq1\\), then \\( r(A)=a(A),\\qquad \\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)<\\infty. \\)", "statement": "<p>If an elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\) satisfies \\(a(A)\\leq1\\), then\n\\[\nr(A)=a(A),\\qquad \\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the Gross–Zagier–Kolyvagin theorem, using modularity [12, 17, 4].</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Proposition 2.4, “Isogeny invariance.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose \\(A\\) and \\(A'\\) are isogenous elliptic curves over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of analytic rank at most one.", "statement": "<p>Suppose \\(A\\) and \\(A'\\) are isogenous elliptic curves over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), of analytic rank at most one. Then\n\\[\n\\frac{Q_A}{\\#\\Sha(A/\\mathbb Q)}\n=\n\\frac{Q_{A'}}{\\#\\Sha(A'/\\mathbb Q)}.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(X_p(A)=X_p(A')\\).</p><p>Over a number field, the corresponding analytic-to-arithmetic BSD ratio is likewise invariant under isogeny when the Tate–Shafarevich groups are finite, and is compatible with products and restriction of scalars.</p>", "proof": "<p>These are Cassels’s arithmetic-volume identities and Milne’s restriction-of-scalars comparison [6, 19]. The isogeny kernel and cokernel factors cancel in the combined expression involving periods, the full point lattice, torsion, components, and finite Tate–Shafarevich group. The analytic \\(L\\)-function is isogeny invariant. Lemma 1.3 supplies finiteness in the rational low-rank applications, and finiteness passes to the products and restrictions used here. No conjectural equality of either ratio with one is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Theorem 2.5, “Analytic twist densities.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\), among nonzero squarefree integers \\(d\\), ordered by \\(|d|\\), the twists \\(A^d\\) of analytic rank zero and one each have density \\(1/2\\).", "statement": "<p>For every elliptic curve \\(A/\\mathbb Q\\), among nonzero squarefree integers \\(d\\), ordered by \\(|d|\\), the twists \\(A^d\\) of analytic rank zero and one each have density \\(1/2\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\{d:a(A^d)\\geq2\\}\n\\]\nhas density zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the external input [22, Theorem 1.2]. Its use requires no uniform estimate as \\(A\\) varies: one first chooses one discriminant and then applies the theorem to two fixed curves.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 2.6, “Compatible auxiliary fields.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) have conductor \\(N\\) and analytic rank \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), and fix an odd prime \\(p\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) have conductor \\(N\\) and analytic rank \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), and fix an odd prime \\(p\\). There are coprime negative odd fundamental discriminants \\(D,D'&lt;-4\\) such that, for\n\\[\nK=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt D),\\qquad\nF=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{DD'}),\\qquad\nL=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt D,\\sqrt{D'}),\n\\]\nthe following hold:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\text{every prime dividing }2Np\\text{ splits in both imaginary quadratic fields},\\\\\n&amp;\\left(\\frac D{|D'|}\\right)=1,\\\\\n&amp;a(E^D)=a(E^{D'})=1-r,\\qquad a(E^{DD'})=r,\\\\\n&amp;L/F\\text{ is CM and unramified at every finite place},\\\\\n&amp;\\mu(L)=\\{\\pm1\\},\\qquad L\\cap\\mathbb Q(E[p])=\\mathbb Q.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWhen \\(E\\) has CM, \\(L\\) may be required not to contain its CM field. Finitely many additional splitting conditions and exclusions of quadratic fields may be imposed.</p><p>Both \\(L(E/K,s)\\) and \\(L(E^{D'}/K,s)\\) have a simple zero at \\(s=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Negative odd fundamental discriminants in an admissible congruence class have positive density. Prescribe splitting at \\(2Np\\), the additional splitting conditions, and finitely many field exclusions. Lemma 1.5 permits a choice of \\(D\\) with \\(a(E^D)\\leq1\\).</p><p>Keep \\(D\\) fixed and choose \\(D'\\) prime to \\(D\\), subject also to\n\\[\nD'\\equiv-1\\pmod{|D|}.\n\\]\nThese conditions still contain admissible classes of positive density. Since \\(|D'|\\equiv1\\pmod{|D|}\\), quadratic reciprocity gives the required Jacobi symbol. Apply Lemma 1.5 to the two fixed curves \\(E\\) and \\(E^D\\); the union of their exceptional sets has density zero. Thus\n\\[\na(E^{D'})\\leq1,\\qquad a(E^{DD'})\\leq1.\n\\]\nFor a fundamental discriminant \\(b\\) prime to \\(N\\), the root-number multiplier is \\(\\chi_b(-N)\\). The sign and splitting prescriptions make it \\(-1\\) for \\(D,D'\\) and \\(+1\\) for \\(DD'\\), giving the asserted ranks and the two simple-zero factorizations.</p><p>The three quadratic discriminants are \\(D,D',DD'\\), and\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{disc}L|=D^2(D')^2=\\operatorname{disc}(F)^2.\n\\]\nHence the relative discriminant of \\(L/F\\) is trivial. Excluding the finitely many quadratic subfields responsible for additional roots of unity gives \\(\\mu(L)=\\{\\pm1\\}\\).</p><p>A nontrivial intersection of \\(L\\) with \\(\\mathbb Q(E[p])\\) contains one of the three quadratic subfields of \\(L\\). The finite extension \\(\\mathbb Q(E[p])\\) has only finitely many quadratic subfields, so these possibilities, and the CM field when relevant, can be excluded during the choices. Finite exclusions do not change the density argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.8, “Full-index discrepancy identity.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt D)\\), where \\(D&lt;-4\\) is odd fundamental, \\((D,N)=1\\), and every prime dividing the conductor \\(N\\) of \\(E\\) splits in \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(L(E/K,s)\\) has a simple zero at \\(s=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an oriented ideal of \\(\\mathcal O_K\\) of norm \\(N\\) with cyclic quotient. Let \\(P_X\\in J_0(N)(K)\\) be the sum, once over the ideal class group, of the associated maximal-order Heegner divisor minus \\(\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a rational modular parametrization \\(\\varphi:X_0(N)\\to E\\), with \\(\\varphi(\\infty)=O\\), put\n  \\[\n  P_K=\\varphi_*P_X,\\qquad\n  \\varphi^*\\omega_E=c_Ef(q)\\frac{dq}{q},\\qquad\n  A_E=\\int_{E(\\mathbb C)}|\\omega_E\\wedge\\overline{\\omega_E}|.\n  \\]\n  The rational number \\(c_E\\neq0\\) is retained exactly.</p><p class=\"li\">The absolute height \\(H_{\\mathrm{abs}}\\) is the height convention of Theorem 1 over number fields.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{Q_E}{\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)}\n\\frac{Q_{E^D}}{\\#\\Sha(E^D/\\mathbb Q)}\n=\n\\frac{[E(K):\\mathbb ZP_K]^2}\n{c_E^2\\prod_\\ell c_\\ell(E)^2\\,\\#\\Sha(E/K)}.\n\\]\nThe index includes torsion. Consequently, if\n\\[\nn_P=v_p([E(K)/E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}:\\mathbb Z\\overline P_K]),\n\\qquad\n\\tau_g=v_p(\\#E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nX_p(E)+X_p(E^D)\n=\n2(n_P+\\tau_g)-2v_p(c_E)\n-2\\sum_\\ell v_p(c_\\ell(E))-v_p(\\#\\Sha(E/K)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(A=\\operatorname{Res}_{K/\\mathbb Q}E_K\\). It is rationally isogenous to \\(E\\times E^D\\), with point group \\(E(K)\\), Tate–Shafarevich group \\(\\Sha(E/K)\\), and \\(L\\)-function \\(L(E/K,s)\\).</p><p>Its Néron period is \\(A_E/\\sqrt{|D|}\\): an integral quadratic basis in restriction of the differential gives real Jacobian \\(1/\\sqrt{|D|}\\). The differential stays minimal over \\(K\\), since bad primes split and good reduction stays good. Its component product is \\(\\prod_\\ell c_\\ell(E)^2\\). At ramified good primes the special restriction fiber is an extension of the good special fiber by a connected vector group, so there is no extra component factor.</p><p>Let \\(P_0\\) generate \\(E(K)/E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}\\), and write \\(P_K=mP_0\\) modulo torsion. Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Reg}_A=2H_{\\mathrm{abs}}(P_0),\\qquad\n\\frac{2H_{\\mathrm{abs}}(P_K)}{\\operatorname{Reg}_A}=m^2,\n\\qquad\n[E(K):\\mathbb ZP_K]=|m|\\#E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}.\n\\]\nDivide Lemma 2.1’s height formula by this arithmetic volume. Proposition 1’s isogeny and restriction-of-scalars comparison identifies the result with the product of the two rational discrepancies. Taking \\(v_p\\) gives the second identity. This real identity does not use a two-primary BSD equality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Theorem 2.7, “Split Gross–Zagier normalization.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 2, let \\((f,f)_N\\) be the unnormalized Petersson integral.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 2, let \\((f,f)_N\\) be the unnormalized Petersson integral. On the Jacobian use the Poincaré pairing and the orthogonal projector to the rational newform factor, with restricted polarization; denote its absolute diagonal by \\(H_{\\mathrm{abs},f}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{|D|}}{8\\pi^2(f,f)_N}L'(f/K,1)\n=\n2H_{\\mathrm{abs},f}(P_X),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nL'(E/K,1)\n=\n\\frac{A_E}{\\sqrt{|D|}\\,c_E^2}\\,2H_{\\mathrm{abs}}(P_K).\n\\]\nIn the simple-zero case \\(P_K\\) is nontorsion.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first equality is the conductor-one split Gross–Zagier formula in the normalization of [12, Chapters I and V], with the conversion recorded in [24, Section 2]. The projection formula and\n\\[\n(\\deg\\varphi)A_E=8\\pi^2c_E^2(f,f)_N\n\\]\ngive the second equality.</p><p>The relative height over \\(K\\) is \\(2H_{\\mathrm{abs}}\\). The canonical height of the origin divisor is \\(H_{\\mathrm{abs}}/2\\), while its Poincaré-pairing diagonal is \\(H_{\\mathrm{abs}}\\). This accounts for the displayed factors without an additional halving.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Integral cohomological models, orthogonality, and evaluation.", "statement": "<p>Integral cohomological models, orthogonality, and evaluation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an odd prime \\(p\\), put \\(T=T_pE\\), \\(V=T\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z_p}\\mathbb Q_p\\), and identify \\(T\\) with \\(T^*(1)\\) by the Weil pairing.</p><p class=\"li\">Frobenius is arithmetic. Character corestriction is an unnormalized sum.</p><p class=\"li\">For a number field \\(M_0\\), support \\(S\\), and finite free coefficient module \\(M\\), write\n  \\[\n  G(M)=C^\\bullet(G_{M_0,S},M),\\qquad L_v(M)=C^\\bullet((M_0)_v,M).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For local maps \\(U_v\\to L_v\\), define\n  \\[\n  C(U,M)=\\operatorname{fib}\\!\\left(G(M)\\to\\bigoplus_v\\operatorname{cone}(U_v\\to L_v)\\right).\n  \\]\n  Full means the identity condition and strict means zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Model rings are complete power-series rings over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\), obtained from Artin stages as in Lemma 3.1 below.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The arithmetic diagrams used in the paper admit bounded finite free models with derived base change, preserving restriction, inflation, localization cones, cup products, invariant maps, conjugation, duality, and specified homotopies. Exact complementary local conditions give Selmer duality with shift \\(-3\\). Degree-one classes on field fibers, including residual fraction-field fibers, admit the evaluation detection described in Lemma 3.3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 3.1 to the global-local diagrams with the boundedness verification in its proof. The localization and exact complement calculations of Lemma 3.2 preserve the arithmetic duality maps, rather than merely cohomological dimensions. Lemma 3.3 supplies the evaluation interpretation required for subsequent moving-prime constructions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Finite models and marked operations.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Consider continuous-cochain diagrams over finite local Artin rings of \\(p\\)-power characteristic, with finite free coefficients and compatible coefficient lifts.", "statement": "<p>Consider continuous-cochain diagrams over finite local Artin rings of \\(p\\)-power characteristic, with finite free coefficients and compatible coefficient lifts. Assume residual cohomology dimensions are uniformly bounded and vanish outside a uniformly bounded range whenever a bounded model is required.</p><p>Then the diagrams admit bounded finite free models with derived base change, retaining the marked operations of Proposition 3.</p><p>If every fixed Artin precision of a complete local ring \\(R\\), with finite residue field, occurs at the stages, a fixed nonprincipal ultrafilter gives compatible matrix limits over \\(R\\). All marked identities survive arbitrary derived base change, including residue fields of primes containing \\(p\\). Stabilizing fixed-group diagrams compute actual cohomology. Finite enlargements may retain the previous models and markings.</p>", "proof": "<p>Continuous functions with free coefficients over an Artin ring \\(A\\) are filtered colimits of finite free modules indexed by finite clopen partitions, hence flat. Flat modules over local Artin rings are free: lift a residual basis, use nilpotence for surjectivity, and use flatness and nilpotence to kill the kernel.</p><p>Split the residual complex into cohomology and contractible disks, and lift graded bases. For the disk differential \\(d_0\\), retain \\(i,q,h\\) with\n\\[\nh^2=hi=qh=0,\\qquad 1-iq=d_0h+hd_0.\n\\]\nWriting \\(d=d_0+\\epsilon\\), use\n\\[\nI=(1+h\\epsilon)^{-1}i,\\quad\nQ=q(1+\\epsilon h)^{-1},\\quad\nH=h(1+\\epsilon h)^{-1},\\quad\nd_{\\mathrm{small}}=q\\epsilon(1+h\\epsilon)^{-1}i.\n\\]\nThese are finite series in the nilpotent maximal ideal. They satisfy the contraction identities and leave ranks equal to the bounded residual cohomology dimensions. Insert \\(I,Q\\) to transfer maps and \\(H\\) to transfer composition homotopies; tensor contractions transfer cups and their homotopies. Form and contract cones.</p><p>Functions lift across coefficient quotients, allowing compatible choices. At fixed precision the matrix sets are finite, so ultrafilter limits preserve every finite identity. Cone identifications preserve triangles, and contracted duality comparison cones preserve duality. Free models make ordinary tensor derived tensor.</p><p>For a fixed group, compare the models at each precision with contractions of actual cochains. Compact inverse limits have no \\(\\varprojlim^1\\) contribution. To enlarge diagrams, keep the old contractions and transfer the new maps, slowing precision if necessary.</p><p>For the diagrams in use, residual representations lie in a fixed finite list and support cardinalities are bounded. After a fixed extension trivializing residual coefficients and containing \\(\\mu_p\\), Kummer theory bounds degree one by fixed unit and class groups and the number of allowed valuations. Inflation–restriction, duality, and odd-primary cohomological-dimension bounds give the other bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Exact local complements.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(U_v\\to L_v\\) and \\(U'_v\\to L'_v\\) be paired perfect local maps with a specified nullhomotopy of their restricted cup product.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(U_v\\to L_v\\) and \\(U'_v\\to L'_v\\) be paired perfect local maps with a specified nullhomotopy of their restricted cup product. Assume\n\\[\nL'_v/U'_v\\simeq U_v^\\vee[-2].\n\\]\nThen their Selmer complexes are perfect duals with shift \\(-3\\), and localization boundaries for nested conditions are adjoint. These statements hold over the model rings and after localization or derived specialization.</p><p>At a non-\\(p\\) unramified place,\n\\[\nU_v^{\\mathrm{ur}}=[M\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{Frob}_v-1}M]\n\\]\nin degrees \\(0,1\\); its quotient is residue cohomology on \\(M(-1)\\) in degrees \\(1,2\\). After inverting \\(p\\), for fixed inertia action and varying unramified scalar, the corresponding complexes use \\(V^{I_v}\\) and \\(V_{I_v}(-1)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Poitou–Tate pairs the compact-support global complex with the unrestricted dual global complex with shift \\(-3\\). Local Tate duality has shift \\(-2\\). Dualizing\n\\[\nG_c\\longrightarrow C(U)\\longrightarrow\\bigoplus_vU_v\n\\]\nand using the local equivalences identifies the dual fiber with \\(C(U')\\), and identifies the boundary maps as adjoints.</p><p>At finite precision retain cups, invariants, compact traces, and the reciprocity homotopy. Perfectness is checked residually and lifted by contracting the comparison cone. Unramified cup nullhomotopies factor through residue cochains.</p><p>Localization and purity give the unramified comparisons with their maps. Locally, take invariants under the pro-prime-to-\\(p\\) inertia kernel, then use the procyclic tame \\(p\\)-inertia resolution. A generator change \\(z\\mapsto z^b\\) acts in degree one by \\((z^b-1)/(z-1)\\); these geometric sums tend to one under the prescribed moving-prime limits.</p><p>After inverting \\(p\\), constant inertia linear algebra gives conditions containing all \\(H^0\\) and injecting into local \\(H^1\\); local duality and Euler characteristic identify exact complements. Conjugate transport puts complements at conjugate places. Shapiro compatibility uses corestriction without a quadratic-degree factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Evaluation detection.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(G_R(M)\\) be an unrestricted limiting global model of Lemma 3.1.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(G_R(M)\\) be an unrestricted limiting global model of Lemma 3.1. After any field specialization, including\n\\[\nR\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Frac}(\\mathbb F_p[[\\mathbf u]]),\n\\]\nits degree-one cohomology injects into abstract crossed-homomorphism classes on the product of the stage absolute Galois groups, with action obtained by entrywise limits before specialization. Invariants are detected similarly.</p><p>The assertion applies to a Selmer complex when its local conditions contain all local \\(H^0\\) and inject into local field \\(H^1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep the finite-model inclusions into actual cochains. At residual precision, a bounded list of group elements detects invariants and whether a model cocycle is a coboundary \\(ga-a\\). Mark its evaluation map to a two-term complex \\([M\\to M^{\\mathrm{list}}]\\). This is an isomorphism on residual \\(H^0\\) and injective on residual \\(H^1\\). The minimal cone has zero terms in degrees at most zero, a property preserved by every field base change.</p><p>Evaluation matrices for sequences of elements have compatible limits, and their cocycle, coboundary, and conjugation identities survive. The Selmer assertion follows from localization.</p><p>On a normal joint kernel acting trivially on coefficients, restriction gives additive equivariant evaluations. It detects classes if the quotient has \\(H^1=0\\); a central scalar differing from one invertibly gives this vanishing. In characteristic zero, the same vanishing for arbitrary sequences in a fixed deep \\(\\operatorname{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) follows by diagonal conjugation, the unipotent power formula, and Gaussian factorization. In a non-CM Tate image, repeated derived groups remove competing bounded-derived-length data while retaining a deep special-linear subgroup.</p><p>For absolutely simple coefficients with scalar endomorphisms, evaluations of independent detected classes span their full tuple of coefficient spaces; otherwise equivariant span gives a scalar relation. This holds jointly for pairwise nonisomorphic simples.</p><p>Finite evaluation and arithmetic tables factor through finite quotients and can be realized by Chebotarev when splitting prescriptions are compatible. Inflation preserves old evaluations. Cup-primitive calculations use transferred arithmetic invariant identities, not reciprocity for an abstract product group. Fixed denominators may be cleared only after inverting \\(p\\) in characteristic-zero tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Determinant switches and Kummer volumes.", "statement": "<p>Determinant switches and Kummer volumes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a perfect complex \\(C\\), use the inverse determinant\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D(C)=\\bigotimes_i(\\det C^i)^{(-1)^{i+1}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Over a DVR \\(A\\), \\(d(C,y)\\) is the valuation of a generic tensor \\(y\\) relative to an integral generator.</p><p class=\"li\">Torsion of length \\(b\\) in degree \\(i\\) contributes \\((-1)^{i+1}b\\); in particular, for a generically acyclic square complex in degrees \\(1,2\\),\n  \\[\n  d(C,1)=-v(\\det d^1).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Determinants multiply in exact triangles.</p><p class=\"li\">In the switching diagrams,\n  \\[\n  U_-\\subset U_f,U_s\\subset U_+,\\quad\n  U_f\\cap U_s=U_-,\\quad U_f+U_s=U_+,\n  \\]\n  with split degree-one quotients \\(F,S,F\\oplus S\\). The lower condition retains all \\(H^0\\), the upper omits \\(H^2\\), and mixed pairings are perfect.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line and square switches of Lemmas 4.1–4.2 preserve the specified determinant volumes and lower residual dimensions. The strict–finite comparison is Lemma 4.3. At actual scalar specializations, full integral Kummer conditions, including torsion, have the local and global volumes of Lemmas 4.4–4.5.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1–4.3 establish the determinant identities directly from localization triangles and exact duality. Lemmas 4.4–4.5 compute the actual local and global Kummer lattices. Retaining torsion and derived specialization is essential to all later central valuations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, “Line switch.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 4, suppose \\(F,S\\) are lines, lower and upper global diagrams have exact complementary duality, and the finite and transverse complexes have amplitude \\([1,2]\\) generically and residually.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 4, suppose \\(F,S\\) are lines, lower and upper global diagrams have exact complementary duality, and the finite and transverse complexes have amplitude \\([1,2]\\) generically and residually. Suppose the finite complex has only a generic degree-one line.</p><p>At a fiber with \\(\\dim H^1(C_f\\otimes k)&gt;1\\), assume nonzero finite evaluation both on a primitive reduction of that line and on some degree-one class of the opposite condition. Switching lowers degree-one dimension by one and retains one generic degree-one line.</p><p>If integral classes \\(Y,Y'\\) satisfy \\(s(Y')=f(Y)\\) under a unit-volume coordinate isomorphism, then\n\\[\nd(C_f,Y)=d(C_s,Y').\n\\]\nSuccessive switches ending at dimension one prove nonnegativity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Nonzero generic finite evaluation makes the lower complex generically acyclic. In the fiber, reciprocity against the opposite nonzero finite evaluation prohibits a transverse contribution; the two localization triangles lower dimension by one.</p><p>The same triangles identify both class tensors through the common lower tensor \\(1\\), with only a unit change of local volume. At terminal dimension one the minimal model is a free degree-one line, so an integral class has nonnegative coordinate valuation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, “Square switch.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 4’s diagram, let \\(F,S\\) be planes.", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 4’s diagram, let \\(F,S\\) be planes. Assume amplitude \\([1,2]\\), exact self-duality of lower and upper diagrams, isotropic pure planes with retained cup nullhomotopies, and perfect mixed pairing. On the common plane let \\(e\\) be unimodular alternating and \\(J\\) an integral involution, with cross pairing \\(e(x,Jy)\\) up to a unit.</p><p>Suppose \\(C_f\\) has generic rank one in degrees one and two, with class \\(Y\\), \\(f(Y)\\neq0\\), and an integral transverse class satisfying \\(s(Y')=Jf(Y)\\) up to a unit. Then \\(C_s\\) has the same generic ranks and\n\\[\nd(C_f,(Y,Y^\\vee))=d(C_s,(Y',(Y')^\\vee)).\n\\]\nHere the dual tensor uses the pairing functional on degree two.</p><p>At a fiber of degree-one dimension greater than one, rank-two finite evaluation, nonzero on a primitive reduction of \\(Y\\), lowers the dimension by two. Ending at dimension one gives nonnegative valuation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Adjunction makes the upper localization image self-annihilating in four coordinates. The two generic classes form an upper degree-one basis. In the dual basis of \\(H^2(C_-)\\), choose a rational complement \\(x\\) to \\(f(Y)\\) whose boundary is the normalized dual of \\(Y'\\). The cross pairing gives a unit value for \\(e(x,Js(Y'))\\); since \\(s(Y')=Jf(Y)\\), the wedge \\(f(Y)\\wedge x\\) has unit plane volume.</p><p>The complement need not be integral: simultaneous multiplication of the two classes by a uniformizer divides the normalized dual and this complement by that uniformizer. Interchanging planes gives the same lower tensor and proves equality of class-functional volumes.</p><p>At a rank-two fiber the upper image is the finite plane, producing the two-dimensional drop. At terminal dimension one the model consists of two free lines with zero differential and unimodular pairing, so the tensor is integral.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.6, “Strict versus finite conditions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At a split pair \\(w,\\bar w\\), let \\(C_*\\) be strict at \\(w\\), full at \\(\\bar w\\), and let \\(C_F\\) impose exactly complementary finite conditions \\(U_w,U_{\\bar w}\\).", "statement": "<p>At a split pair \\(w,\\bar w\\), let \\(C_*\\) be strict at \\(w\\), full at \\(\\bar w\\), and let \\(C_F\\) impose exactly complementary finite conditions \\(U_w,U_{\\bar w}\\). Keep other conditions fixed with conjugate duality.</p><p>Over a DVR, assume \\(U_w\\) is generically a degree-one line, \\(C_*\\) is generically acyclic, and \\(Y\\in H^1(C_F)\\) has nonzero localization. If \\(\\operatorname{loc}_wY=ae\\), then\n\\[\nd(C_F,(Y,Y^\\vee))\n=\nd(C_*,1)+2\\bigl(v(a)+d(U_w,e)\\bigr).\n\\]\nMoreover \\(C_F\\) has one generic line in each of degrees one and two. Conversely, those ranks and nonzero localization imply strict acyclicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the intermediate condition strict at \\(w\\), finite at \\(\\bar w\\), and the two triangles with quotients \\(U_w\\) and \\(U_{\\bar w}^{\\mathrm{full}}/U_{\\bar w}\\simeq U_w^\\vee[-2]\\). The boundary of \\(e^*\\) pairs with \\(Y\\) by \\(a\\). The two comparisons give the factor \\(a^2\\), with dual local lattice volumes. The same nonzero adjoint boundary gives the rank statements and converse.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.7, “Local Haar factors.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the minimal differential, put \\( P_\\ell(1)=L_\\ell(E,1)^{-1},\\qquad \\tau_\\ell=\\operatorname{length}_{\\mathbb Z_p}E(\\mathbb Q_\\ell)[p^\\infty], \\qquad \\log_{\\omega_E}E(\\mathbb Q_p)=p^l\\mathbb Z_p. \\) Then …", "statement": "<p>For the minimal differential, put\n\\[\nP_\\ell(1)=L_\\ell(E,1)^{-1},\\qquad\n\\tau_\\ell=\\operatorname{length}_{\\mathbb Z_p}E(\\mathbb Q_\\ell)[p^\\infty],\n\\qquad\n\\log_{\\omega_E}E(\\mathbb Q_p)=p^l\\mathbb Z_p.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\tau_\\ell=v_p(c_\\ell P_\\ell(1))\\quad(\\ell\\neq p),\n\\qquad\n\\tau_p-l=v_p(c_pP_p(1)).\n\\]\nFor a primitive positive Betti cycle, its period \\(\\Omega_0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Omega_E/\\Omega_0=\\#\\pi_0(E(\\mathbb R)),\n\\]\na \\(p\\)-adic unit.</p>", "proof": "<p>The minimal-differential measure of identity-component points is the nonsingular residue size divided by the residue characteristic, namely \\(P_\\ell(1)\\). Multiplication by the component number gives \\(c_\\ell P_\\ell(1)\\). Away from \\(p\\), the formal subgroup is pro-\\(\\ell\\) and contributes no \\(p\\)-valuation.</p><p>At \\(p\\), formal logarithm identifies a sufficiently small subgroup with an additive lattice. The \\(p\\)-primary logarithm kernel has size \\(p^{\\tau_p}\\), and its image has measure \\(p^{-l}\\). This gives the second formula. Translation across real components gives the period identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.8, “Global Kummer volume.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(K\\) be imaginary quadratic, \\(E(K)\\) have rank one, and \\(\\Sha(E/K)\\) be finite.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(K\\) be imaginary quadratic, \\(E(K)\\) have rank one, and \\(\\Sha(E/K)\\) be finite. Let \\(C_{\\mathrm{Kum}}\\) impose\n\\[\nU_v^{\\mathrm{Kum}}=E(K_v)^\\wedge_p[-1]\\longrightarrow C^\\bullet(K_v,T),\n\\]\nincluding completed-point torsion.</p><p>For nontorsion \\(P\\), put\n\\[\nn_P=v_p([E(K)/E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}:\\mathbb Z\\bar P]),\\quad\n\\tau_g=v_p(\\#E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}),\\quad\ns_K=v_p(\\#\\Sha(E/K)).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nd(C_{\\mathrm{Kum}},(P,P^\\vee))=2n_P+2\\tau_g-s_K.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Derived Kummer reduction has invariant \\(p^n\\)-torsion in degree zero and the finite Kummer image in degree one. Finite Kummer orthogonality and derived base change give exact complements, retaining their cup nullhomotopies.</p><p>Whole-\\(\\Sha\\) finiteness identifies integral degree one with completed global points. The torsion lengths in degrees one, two, and three are respectively \\(\\tau_g,s_K,\\tau_g\\). Duality gives a unimodular free pairing in degrees one and two. Their signed torsion contribution is \\(2\\tau_g-s_K\\), and the point and its pairing functional each contribute \\(n_P\\).</p><p>Locally, compact duality gives\n\\[\n0\\to E(K_v)^\\wedge_p\\to H^1(K_v,T)\n\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(E^\\vee(K_v)^\\wedge_p,\\mathbb Z_p)\\to0.\n\\]\nAt non-\\(p\\) places compact \\(H^1\\) is entirely Kummer. The rational logarithm–dual-exponential pairing is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{inv}_v(z\\cup x)\n=\n\\pm\\operatorname{Tr}_{K_v/\\mathbb Q_p}(\\exp^*z\\cdot\\log x),\n\\]\nincluding bad reduction, without asserting integral exponential bases.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Bounded-series division and specialization.", "statement": "<p>Bounded-series division and specialization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A bounded series has coefficients in a fixed complete DVR, or in its fraction field with one common denominator.</p><p class=\"li\">Besides finite coefficient rings, the paper permits\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V=W(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p),\\qquad\n  \\mathcal V^*=\n  \\varprojlim_j\\prod_{\\mathcal U}(\\mathcal V/p^j)\n  =\n  W\\!\\left(\\prod_{\\mathcal U}\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\right),\n  \\]\n  and fixed finite ramified extensions.</p><p class=\"li\">The variable \\(t\\) is genuine; the variables \\(\\mathbf u\\) are limits of tame character generators \\(1+u_j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>High finite characters detect the integral divisibility of Lemma 5.1. Bounded stage numerators and denominators satisfy the pole-persistence principle of Lemma 5.2. Horizontal divisors admit interior algebraic test points as in Lemma 5.3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemmas 5.1–5.3.</p><p>The additional central-coordinate observation is original Remark 3.12: when generic and specialized cohomology dimensions agree, determinant coordinates specialize through any surviving Gaussian pivots. Bounds on torsion lengths and rank tests keep suitable minors nonzero in the limit, so numerator, denominator, and class coordinates converge through their finite matrix formulas. A particular rational pivot formula need not survive.</p><p>Also, a nonzero bounded one-variable series has, at sufficiently high characters, valuation equal to its minimum coefficient valuation plus the first degree attaining that minimum times \\(v_p(\\theta-1)\\). Unique dominance gives finite-character avoidance in several variables by iteration.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.9, “Division from high characters.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal O\\) be one of the complete integer DVRs above, with uniformizer \\(\\varpi\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal O\\) be one of the complete integer DVRs above, with uniformizer \\(\\varpi\\). Let\n\\[\nL,B\\in\\mathcal O[[t,\\mathbf u]],\\qquad L\\not\\equiv0\\pmod{(\\varpi,t)}.\n\\]\nIf for every sufficiently high finite \\(p\\)-power character \\(\\theta\\) of \\(1+t\\), \\(B(\\theta,\\mathbf u)\\) is divisible by \\(L(\\theta,\\mathbf u)\\) after inverting \\(p\\), then \\(L\\mid B\\) integrally.</p><p>The assertion applies to finite products of divisors and the fixed constant extensions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose integers \\(n_j\\), one equal to one and the others of sufficiently separated \\(p\\)-valuations, so that\n\\[\n1+u_j=(1+x)^{n_j}\n\\]\nseparates a nonzero leading residual monomial. Extend this to a group-power coordinate change. Then \\(L\\) is distinguished in one tame coordinate. Applying this to a product handles finitely many factors; finitely many characteristic-zero rank or nonvanishing requirements can also be retained by avoiding their proper conditions.</p><p>Divide \\(B\\) by the monic Weierstrass polynomial of \\(L\\). At every high \\(\\theta\\), the distinguished degree is unchanged and the quotient module has no \\(p\\)-torsion. Divisibility after inverting \\(p\\) therefore kills the integral remainder. Each bounded coefficient of that remainder vanishes at all high characters, hence is zero by one-variable Weierstrass preparation. The same proof works componentwise for finite coefficient orders embedded in products of DVRs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.10, “Persistence of poles.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose integral stage numerators and denominators in a genuine variable converge coefficientwise along the fixed ultrafilter, with nonzero limiting denominator.", "statement": "<p>Suppose integral stage numerators and denominators in a genuine variable converge coefficientwise along the fixed ultrafilter, with nonzero limiting denominator. If every stage numerator is divisible by its denominator after inverting \\(p\\), then each zero of the limiting denominator in the open unit disk is a zero of the limiting numerator with at least the same multiplicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Coefficientwise convergent integral series converge uniformly on smaller closed disks. Isolate a zero of multiplicity \\(m\\) of the limiting denominator on such a disk. Weierstrass preparation preserves its zero count on an ultrafilter-large set. Stage divisibility forces at least that multiplicity in the limiting numerator. Numerator and denominator are bounded separately; no uniform bound on their stage quotient is required. Fix tame characters before applying this one-variable argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.11, “Points on a horizontal divisor.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a characteristic-zero height-one divisor in a bounded \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-power-series ring, finitely many functions nonzero on the divisor can be kept nonzero at a point with all coordinates in the open unit disk of a …", "statement": "<p>For a characteristic-zero height-one divisor in a bounded \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-power-series ring, finitely many functions nonzero on the divisor can be kept nonzero at a point with all coordinates in the open unit disk of a finite extension of \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A weighted coordinate change makes the prime generator distinguished in one variable. Modulo its Weierstrass polynomial, the norms of the finitely many nonzero functions to the remaining power-series ring are nonzero. Choose the other coordinates avoiding those norms by bounded-series avoidance. A root of the distinguished polynomial is interior and algebraic, giving the point.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 4.2, “Residual concentration.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p&gt;2\\), \\(T=T_pE\\), and \\(R=\\mathbb Z_p[[t,\\mathbf u]]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), assume \\(E\\) is non-CM and let \\(t\\) be cyclotomic; let \\(G\\) be the unrestricted global complex for \\(T\\chi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Over \\(K\\), use Proposition 1’s fields, let \\(t\\) be the free anticyclotomic variable, and let\n  \\[\n  C_w=\\operatorname{fib}(G\\to L_w^{\\mathrm{loc}})\n  \\]\n  be strict at \\(w\\mid p\\), full at the other \\(p\\)-place and at support places. Write \\(L_w\\) for its square differential determinant, and \\(L'_w\\) for the companion determinant.</p><p class=\"li\">The support includes the specified fixed primes and split good ideal-class generators. Each artificial variable comes from a sequence of distinct good primes, tending \\(p\\)-adically to one, with cyclic \\(p\\)-power inertia order tending to infinity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After adjoining finitely many tame variables, the separate rational and imaginary tuples satisfy\n\\[\nG\\otimes_RR_{(p)}\\simeq R_{(p)}[-1],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nL_w,L'_w\\not\\equiv0\\pmod{(p,t)}\n\\qquad(w\\mid p).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At the residual test, each residual elliptic representation is absolutely simple or has two distinct character constituents. Complex conjugation has eigenvalues \\(1,-1\\), including after restriction to \\(G_K\\) by disjointness. Test simple constituents after a finite coefficient extension.</p><p>Every finite local \\(H^0,H^2\\) vanishes: fixed split places have nonconstant Frobenius scalar, nonsplit exceptional companion places have nontrivial inertia, and moving places have their own inertia variable. Away from \\(p\\), Euler characteristic zero gives acyclicity. Selmer \\(H^1\\) injects into unrestricted cohomology.</p><p>Over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), if the dual all-strict dimension is \\(s\\), the paired unrestricted dimension is \\(s+a\\), where \\(a\\) is the negative infinity dimension. Over \\(K\\), opposite one-sided dimensions agree.</p><p>Lemmas 6.4–6.5 provide a linear combination of directions with zero left kernel. For planes this uses the evenness of Lemma 6.3 and a nonsingular alternating matrix. Localize the enlarged residual ring at old fraction-field coefficients and zero new variables. Cancel unit differential blocks. A kernel vector of the first-order differential would pass every necessary cup test, which is impossible. A maximal minor has nonzero initial term and remains nonzero generically, killing the strict space.</p><p>Take the union of the finite direction lists for every constituent, side, and companion. Each retains its own acyclic slice. Long exact sequences of composition filtrations give the result for nonsplit extensions.</p><p>Over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), dual strict vanishing gives \\(H^2=0\\), and Euler characteristic \\(-1\\) gives one degree-one line. Minimality at \\((p,t)\\), then \\((p)\\), gives the asserted free line. Over \\(K\\), one-sided residual \\(H^1,H^2\\) vanish, so each square determinant is nonzero modulo \\((p,t)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Auxiliary tuples.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For any finite list of fixed split support and test primes, including \\(p\\), the auxiliary tuple may be chosen so that:", "statement": "<p>For any finite list of fixed split support and test primes, including \\(p\\), the auxiliary tuple may be chosen so that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Its limiting elliptic Frobenius actions have determinant one and \\(V(\\gamma_j)-1\\) invertible; over \\(K\\), this also holds for the companion.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The fixed weight character \\(\\mathcal C\\) has nontorsion value at each \\(\\gamma_j\\), and the true anticyclotomic exponent there is nonzero.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Frobenius exponent vectors in artificial variables at distinct prescribed rational primes are linearly independent over \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. There are at least two moving primes; each moving inertia maps onto its own cyclic quotient and trivially to the others; all fixed conductors and prescribed exclusions are avoided.</p><p>In the rational problem omit the requirements involving \\(\\mathcal C\\) and the anticyclotomic group.</p>", "proof": "<p>Prescribe power-residue data for the fixed primes over \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Over \\(K\\), use class-group generators, principal power relations, and expressions for the prescribed prime ideals, including conjugates. For a split prime \\(\\mathfrak q\\), a class-number power \\(\\mathfrak q^h=(a_{\\mathfrak q})\\) yields the anticyclotomic radical \\(a_{\\mathfrak q}/\\bar a_{\\mathfrak q}\\); independent valuations give independent radicals.</p><p>Their Kummer image over the cyclotomic tower has full \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-rank. A central cyclotomic element differing from one by a unit gives restriction injectivity modulo powers of \\(p\\); fixed finite extensions lose only a bounded power. Arbitrarily deep power relations would contradict the independent valuations. Class-group invariant-factor relations extend inertia logarithms after fixed divisions, preserving rational span.</p><p>Finite Chebotarev conditions realize the radical tables. Compatibility with elliptic and fixed-character actions follows from the joint analytic image. In the non-CM case a common Lie quotient with radical translations kills \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\). In the CM case, conjugation is trivial on the remaining abelian analytic quotient after a fixed extension but cyclotomic on radical translations; a nontrivial cyclotomic element forces the common Lie quotient to vanish.</p><p>An identity neighborhood remains available with determinant-one projection containing an open special-linear subgroup or norm-one CM torus. Avoid the proper analytic loci for eigenvalue one, torsion weight value, and zero true exponent. Realize increasingly accurate finite tables by Chebotarev. If required, a high power of a near-identity element adjusts \\(v_p(r_{j,i}-1)-n_{j,i}\\) while preserving the tables.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, “Scalar localization.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At \\( k_r=\\operatorname{Frac}(\\mathbb F_p[[\\mathbf u]]),\\qquad t=0, \\) global degree-two localization for the constant roots coefficient is injective.", "statement": "<p>At\n\\[\nk_r=\\operatorname{Frac}(\\mathbb F_p[[\\mathbf u]]),\\qquad t=0,\n\\]\nglobal degree-two localization for the constant roots coefficient is injective. Thus a paired cup product which is locally zero has a global cochain primitive.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Poitou–Tate, the dual localization kernel consists of everywhere locally trivial constant \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-characters. Such characters are everywhere unramified and split at the support, hence are class-group characters killed by the chosen generating prime classes. Over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) the group is zero.</p><p>The scalar diagram is independent of the artificial variables. Tensoring with \\(\\mathbb F_p[[\\mathbf u]]\\) and then its fraction field preserves the injection, equivalently through its retained injectivity cone. Therefore the locally zero scalar class is globally zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4, “Conjugate parity.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an absolutely simple residual elliptic plane over \\(K\\), each one-sided strict space at the residual test of Lemma 6.2 has even dimension.", "statement": "<p>For an absolutely simple residual elliptic plane over \\(K\\), each one-sided strict space at the residual test of Lemma 6.2 has even dimension. The same alternation statement holds at characteristic-zero localizations with unramified conditions at fixed non-\\(p\\) places and constant inertia action.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lift the residual test to the \\((p)\\)-DVR of \\(\\mathbb Z_p[[\\mathbf u]]\\), with \\(t=0\\). Non-\\(p\\) local complexes are contractible. The remaining two-term one-sided problem has conjugate self-duality.</p><p>Its generic fiber is acyclic: central characteristic-zero specialization removes moving singular factors by their invertible Frobenius actions, leaving the fixed-support problem. Proposition 1 gives a single point line with nonzero localization at both \\(p\\)-places; Proposition 4’s strict–finite comparison gives strict acyclicity.</p><p>On the induced coefficient, the pairing is\n\\[\n((a,b),(a',b'))\\longmapsto e(a,b')+e(b,a'),\n\\]\nwhere \\(e\\) is the elliptic alternating form. It is alternating and Tate-equivariant, including under conjugation. Split-coordinate conditions are isotropic; unramified conditions have residue cup nullhomotopies. Newly allowed discriminant places have invariant graphs with the same residue nullhomotopy, since the quadratic index is invertible.</p><p>Transfer graded cup commutativity and cone homotopies integrally. Since \\(2\\) is a unit, antisymmetrization gives a perfect pairing identifying a minimal differential with an alternating matrix:\n\\[\nP(x,dy)=P(dx,y)=-P(y,dx).\n\\]\nResidual and generic nullities of an alternating matrix have the same parity. Generic acyclicity makes residual nullity even. Constant-inertia characteristic-zero comparisons use the same calculation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.5, “Necessary first-order lift test.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Adjoin a new tame logarithm \\(\\lambda\\), locally zero at all old places, with new-prime Frobenius \\(\\gamma\\) acyclic on the old field test.", "statement": "<p>Adjoin a new tame logarithm \\(\\lambda\\), locally zero at all old places, with new-prime Frobenius \\(\\gamma\\) acyclic on the old field test. Let \\(x\\) be an old strict class, \\(d\\) a paired opposite class, and choose bilinearly a primitive\n\\[\ndu=x\\cup d.\n\\]\nIf \\(x\\) lifts with derivative \\(dx_1=-\\lambda\\cup x\\), put\n\\[\nA=(M_x(\\gamma)-1)^{-1}x(\\gamma).\n\\]\nThen the scalar Frobenius evaluation of \\(u-A\\cup d\\) vanishes, up to a common nonzero sign or coordinate factor. Over \\(K\\), the necessary equation is its difference with the conjugate evaluation. The test is linear in the direction and bilinear in \\(x,d\\), over residual and characteristic-zero testing fields.</p>", "proof": "<p>The primitive exists by Lemma 6.2: at \\(p\\) one class is strict, and elsewhere cups vanish through acyclic or residue complexes.</p><p>Apply the arithmetic invariant sum to the closed cochain\n\\[\nx_1\\cup d-\\lambda\\cup u\n\\]\nwith compatible signs. Old contributions vanish because \\(\\lambda=0\\) and the old local conditions annihilate the remaining cup. At the new place, trivialize \\(x\\) by \\(A\\) in the residue complex. The remaining unramified scalar cochain is \\(u-A\\cup d\\); its cup with the normalized tame direction has invariant its Frobenius value. Conjugate logarithms have opposite signs. Cross-prime contributions vanish for combinations of new directions.</p><p>For residual fields, mark evaluations, root bases, cups, and inflation homotopies. Clear finitely many parameter denominators in \\(\\mathbb F_p[[\\mathbf u]]\\), including primitive and inverse-Frobenius denominators. Work over\n\\[\n\\mathbb F_p[[\\mathbf u]]/(\\mathbf u)^b\n\\]\nwith first-order new variables. The identities come from actual stage arithmetic with \\(p=0\\); no lifting of arbitrary residual solutions to \\(\\mathbb Z/p^n\\) is asserted. Pass to the limit in \\(b\\), then invert the same parameter denominators. Characteristic-zero tests instead clear fixed scalar denominators and increase arithmetic precision.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.6, “Obstruction span.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose bases of a strict space of dimension \\(s\\) and its paired opposite space of dimension \\(s+a\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), or \\(s\\) over \\(K\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose bases of a strict space of dimension \\(s\\) and its paired opposite space of dimension \\(s+a\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), or \\(s\\) over \\(K\\). The necessary lift matrices from finitely many new directions span:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Mat}_{s,s+a}(k_r)\n\\]\nin the rational problem,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Mat}_{s,s}(k_r)\n\\]\nfor an imaginary reducible constituent, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Alt}_s(k_r)\n\\]\nfor an absolutely simple imaginary plane.</p><p>The prescribed Tate actions, radical data, old character actions, and new-prime acyclicity can be retained.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the termwise joint kernel \\(J\\) of the Tate and old character actions, classes have additive evaluations. They are detected there because old moving inertia supplies a central scalar differing invertibly from one on the quotient.</p><p>Independent classes in simple coefficients span full evaluation tuples. Opposite twists are nonisomorphic on old inertia; the reducible imaginary problem also has distinct elliptic characters and conjugate evaluations.</p><p>Multiply a Frobenius address by \\([v,w']\\), with \\(v,w'\\in J\\). This preserves Tate, character, and radical data: the radical actions are translations in an abelian group. Class evaluations on the commutator vanish, so the \\(A\\)-correction is unchanged. The primitive changes by\n\\[\n-e(x(v),d(w'))+e(x(w'),d(v)).\n\\]\nJoint spanning and bilinearity give elementary rectangular or square matrices. For an absolutely simple plane, take opposite classes to be conjugates; subtracting conjugate evaluations gives elementary skew matrices \\(E_{ij}-E_{ji}\\).</p><p>Only finite linear span, not realization of every vector by a single evaluation, is needed. Finitely many commutators suffice, and Chebotarev realizes their finite tables with common coordinate markings.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Further tame directions at horizontal tests.", "statement": "<p>Further tame directions at horizontal tests.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use an imaginary tuple satisfying Proposition 6, with at least two old artificial variables.</p><p class=\"li\">A horizontal field test is the derived fiber at\n  \\[\n  k(\\mathfrak p)=\\operatorname{Frac}(R/\\mathfrak p)\n  \\]\n  for a height-one prime \\(\\mathfrak p\\) not containing \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Under enlargement \\(R\\subset R[[\\mathbf v]]\\), retain the old test by localization at \\((\\mathfrak p,\\mathbf v)\\) and specialization \\(\\mathbf v=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Residual concentration persists under the enlargements of Lemma 7.1. For finitely many horizontal tests, new directions can simultaneously be chosen Frobenius-acyclic and retain the primitive-cup and commutator-spanning calculations of Proposition 6.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 gives the integral residual assertion. Lemma 7.2 gives simultaneous characteristic-zero tests while preserving old diagrams and the hypotheses needed for further enlargements.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.7, “Preservation under enlargement.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Proposition 6’s nonzero reductions modulo \\((p,t)\\) persist after adjoining tame primes whose Frobenius has nonzero exponent in preceding artificial variables.", "statement": "<p>Proposition 6’s nonzero reductions modulo \\((p,t)\\) persist after adjoining tame primes whose Frobenius has nonzero exponent in preceding artificial variables. Setting the new variables to zero recovers the old diagram by unramified inflation, with full new-place singular factors retained; those factors have nonzero residual reduction.</p>", "proof": "<p>At the old residual test the new Frobenius scalar involves a nonconstant old variable, so its Frobenius-minus-one determinants are nonzero and its local complex is acyclic. The full-to-unramified triangle multiplies the determinant by a nonzero residual singular factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.8, “Directions at horizontal tests.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For finitely many horizontal field tests as above, finitely many new tame directions, locally zero on all old support, can be chosen so that their local complexes are acyclic at every test and the necessary lift …", "statement": "<p>For finitely many horizontal field tests as above, finitely many new tame directions, locally zero on all old support, can be chosen so that their local complexes are acyclic at every test and the necessary lift matrices retain their commutator-spanning property. The construction respects further field extension and primitive unramified conditions at fixed non-\\(p\\) places.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a height-one test, two independent old variables cannot both have root-of-unity values; at least one inertia scalar has infinite order. Varying new-prime old exponents along that coordinate excludes only boundedly many integer choices for each elliptic eigenvalue exception. A sufficiently large finite grid avoids the finite list of exceptions, retaining the radical conditions and nonzero old exponent.</p><p>Exact unramified orthogonality uses Proposition 3. New logarithms vanish at old places, so old cup and first-order contributions vanish through residue nullhomotopies. Constant scalar localization remains injective after specialization. The marked primitive calculation gives the lift identity at each field.</p><p>Evaluations on the joint kernel are detected by the central old inertia scalar, and the commutator formula preserves the prescriptions. Take the union of separate finite direction lists rather than prescribing independent matrices with one prime. Clear finitely many denominators and slow precision to retain all identities.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Integral Siegel classes, norms, and their period coordinate.", "statement": "<p>Integral Siegel classes, norms, and their period coordinate.</p><p>Original labels incorporated: Proposition 5.2, “Integrality and traces,” and Proposition 5.4, “The period coordinate.”</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p&gt;2\\) and a non-CM curve \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\), replacing it within its isogeny class by the quotient of the optimal \\(J_1(N)\\)-quotient by geometric cusp differences.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(A=p^k\\), put \\(L_0=NA\\), and let \\(\\Pi\\) be the \\(A\\)-degeneracy followed by the quotient map to \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a\\) be the primitive invariant Betti cycle with \\(\\Omega_0=\\int_a\\omega_E&gt;0\\), and let \\(\\delta_\\xi\\) be the Poincaré dual of \\(\\Pi_*\\xi\\{0,\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Square order means \\(n_1=n_2=M\\), \\(L_0\\mid M\\). Unfilled rectangular order means \\(n_1=M,n_2=bM\\), with \\(b=N/p^{v_p(N)}\\), \\((b,M)=1\\), \\(p^{v_p(L_0)}\\mid M\\), and lower row \\((Ns,1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Require \\(v_p(M)\\geq6\\), and choose smoothing integers \\(c,d&gt;1\\), prime to \\(6pn_2\\), congruent to one modulo \\(L_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The class \\(z_\\nu\\) is constructed from the unnormalized traces of Kummer cups of Kato’s normalized units at independent division lifts, twisted by the inverse Weil-root basis and pushed by Lemma 8.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These classes are integral away from \\(pn_2\\infty\\) and compatible with division traces and growth of the \\(p\\)-part of \\(M\\). A fresh good prime \\(\\ell\\) has rational norm multiplier\n\\[\n1-\\frac{a_\\ell(f)[\\ell]}{\\ell}+\\frac{[\\ell]^2}{\\ell}.\n\\]\nFilling a complete prime power \\(q_0^a\\mid b\\) into the first row gives\n\\[\nz_{\\mathrm{filled}}\n=\n\\left(1-\\frac{a_{q_0}(f)\\nu(q_0)}{q_0}\\right)z_{\\mathrm{before}}.\n\\]\nThe trace maps themselves are integral.</p><p>For every finite even character \\(\\nu\\) of conductor dividing \\(M\\),\n\\[\n\\exp^*(z_\\nu)\n=\n\\pm\\Delta_\\nu\\frac{L^{(M)}(f,\\nu,1)}{\\Omega_0}\n\\,\\delta_\\xi(a)\\,\\omega_E,\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_\\nu=(c^2-c\\nu(c)^{-1})(d^2-d\\nu(d)^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe sign is common across symbols. In square order with \\(A=1\\), an integral symbol combination has evaluation one. In rectangular order some \\(A,\\xi\\) has nonzero evaluation.</p>", "proof": "<p>At precision \\(p^j\\), replace both orders by \\(p^jn_1,p^jn_2\\), take independent division lifts \\(P',Q'\\), and trace\n\\[\n({}_cg_{P'})\\cup({}_dg_{Q'})\n\\otimes e_{p^j}(n_1P',n_2Q')^\\vee.\n\\]\nFurther division is Cartesian. Distribution, base change, and the projection formula give compatibility, with matching lower roots and marks. The functions and inverses are integral away from level: their Tate leading terms are signs, roots of unity, and powers of \\(1-\\zeta\\), all units away from level. Arithmetic edge maps commute with traces and the integral map of Lemma 8.1.</p><p>For a fresh good \\(\\ell\\), subtract division tuples whose \\(\\ell\\)-parts lie in a common line and add back \\(\\ell\\) times the zero pair. Quotient isogenies give the Hecke term \\(a_\\ell[\\ell]/\\ell\\), and the added-back multiplication isogeny gives \\([\\ell]^2/\\ell\\). Rectangular row filling subtracts the branches of \\(U_{q_0}\\), giving the stated linear factor, including \\(q_0=2\\).</p><p>Apply the analytic trace identity [24, Section 4, equation (K5)] to Lemma 8.2. The rescaled lattice sums cancel \\(M^{-2}\\), smoothing row reindexing gives \\(\\Delta_\\nu\\), and unfolding over indices prime to \\(M\\) gives \\(L^{(M)}(f,\\nu,1)\\). Relative de Rham push has zero cusp-function coordinates. Writing\n\\[\n\\delta_\\xi=s_0\\omega_E+\\bar s_0\\overline{\\omega_E}\n\\]\ngives \\(s_0+\\bar s_0=\\delta_\\xi(a)/\\Omega_0\\), proving the coordinate. Local duality uses field trace, not an orbit average. Fill and rationally cancel the nonzero bad-prime linear factors for rectangular order.</p><p>Lemma 8.1 gives square evaluation one. For rectangular nonvanishing, choose upper row \\((1,0)\\); Fricke sends the degenerated path to a path from \\(\\infty\\) to \\(-s/p^k\\). Rohrlich’s finite-support nonvanishing and primitive Gauss–Mellin imply that some path has nonzero imaginary part, hence \\(\\delta_\\xi(a)\\neq0\\).</p><p>Finally, non-CM open image bounds the exponent of \\((V/T)^{G_M}\\) uniformly over the abelian fields in use. Thus one fixed multiplier kills torsion norm errors and descent ambiguities.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “The relative lattice.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Relative pullback and Poincaré duality give an integral Galois map \\( H^1(Y_1(L_0),\\mathbb Z_p)(1)\\longrightarrow T_pE. \\) For \\(A=1\\), an integral combination \\(\\delta=\\sum_\\xi n_\\xi\\delta_\\xi\\) of finitely many …", "statement": "<p>Relative pullback and Poincaré duality give an integral Galois map\n\\[\nH^1(Y_1(L_0),\\mathbb Z_p)(1)\\longrightarrow T_pE.\n\\]\nFor \\(A=1\\), an integral combination \\(\\delta=\\sum_\\xi n_\\xi\\delta_\\xi\\) of finitely many symbols satisfies \\(\\delta(a)=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The connected optimal kernel makes integral homology surject onto the optimal elliptic lattice. Quotienting by cusp differences enlarges that lattice exactly by lifts of cusp images; relative cusp paths supply the added generators. Thus\n\\[\nH_1(X_1(N)(\\mathbb C),D_N;\\mathbb Z)\\to H_1(E(\\mathbb C),\\mathbb Z)\n\\]\nis onto. Manin edges generate the relative group. The invariant sublattice is saturated and \\(a\\) primitive, so unimodularity gives a symbol combination evaluating to one. Relative pullback and Poincaré duality give the integral étale map, without a Hecke projector.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Full-level calibration.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a nonzero torsion label \\(P=[x\\tau+y]\\), let \\( E_P= -\\frac1{2\\pi i} \\left. \\sum_{(m,n)\\in(x,y)+\\mathbb Z^2} \\frac{(\\operatorname{Im}\\tau)^w} {(m\\tau+n)|m\\tau+n|^{2w}} \\right|_{w=0}, \\) using regular continuation.", "statement": "<p>For a nonzero torsion label \\(P=[x\\tau+y]\\), let\n\\[\nE_P=\n-\\frac1{2\\pi i}\n\\left.\n\\sum_{(m,n)\\in(x,y)+\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\frac{(\\operatorname{Im}\\tau)^w}\n{(m\\tau+n)|m\\tau+n|^{2w}}\n\\right|_{w=0},\n\\]\nusing regular continuation.</p><p>In square order, before elliptic projection, the dual exponential of the traced Kummer cup is\n\\[\n\\pm M^{-2}(c^2E_P-cE_{cP})(d^2E_Q-dE_{dQ})\\frac{dq}{q}.\n\\]\nThe sign is common to determinant components.</p>", "proof": "<p>Initially impose \\(c,d\\equiv1\\pmod M\\). Apply [15, Theorem 9.5] with \\(k=2\\), \\(r=r'=1\\), equal orders \\(M,M\\), and \\(p\\mid M\\). The symmetric power has degree zero, and the negative Tate moment is exactly the inverse determinant basis used in the construction. Kato’s theorem gives the two weight-one brackets; residues determine the scalar.</p><p>For\n\\[\n\\Theta(t,q)=(1-t)\\prod_{h\\geq1}(1-q^ht)(1-q^h/t),\n\\]\nthe normalized Tate function is\n\\[\n{}_c\\theta(t)=\n(-1)^{(c-1)/2}q^{(c^2-1)/12}t^{-c(c-1)/2}\n\\frac{\\Theta(t,q)^{c^2}}{\\Theta(t^c,q)}.\n\\]\nPeriodicity, divisor, and norms fix this normalization. Put\n\\[\nJ_c(x)=c\\lfloor cx\\rfloor-\\frac{c(c-1)}2\n=c^2B_1(x)-cB_1(\\{cx\\}).\n\\]\nAt \\(t=q^x\\zeta\\), the leading coefficient is \\(\\pm\\zeta^{J_c(x)}\\).</p><p>Choose the cusp \\(P=q^{1/M}\\), \\(Q=q^{1/M}\\zeta_M^u\\), and put \\(M_j=p^jM\\). Division cusp orbits have \\(p^{2j}\\) choices, residue degree \\(p^j\\), and width ratio \\(p^j\\). Tame symbols and the inverse-root twist give residual terms\n\\[\n\\pm J_c(x)J_d(y)\\frac{\\chi_{\\mathrm{cy}}-1}{M_j}.\n\\]\nTransfer and the exponential series give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\chi_{\\mathrm{cy}}^{p^j}-1}{p^jM}\n\\equiv\\frac{\\log\\chi_{\\mathrm{cy}}}{M}\\pmod{p^j}.\n\\]\nBernoulli distribution sums the \\(J\\)-terms, while residue trace has no extra ramification multiplier. The dual exponential sends \\(\\log\\chi_{\\mathrm{cy}}\\) to \\(\\pm1\\). Since \\(dq/q\\) has residue \\(M\\), the scalar is \\(\\pm M^{-2}\\).</p><p>For arbitrary permitted smoothings, compare with \\(c_0\\equiv1\\pmod M\\) through\n\\[\n(c_0^2-[c_0]^*)\\,{}_c\\theta=(c^2-[c]^*)\\,{}_{c_0}\\theta.\n\\]\nThe inverse-root basis contributes \\(c^{-1}\\), giving the differential operator \\(c^2-c\\,\\mathrm{mult}_c^*\\). Rational cancellation proves the general formula without asserting integral divisibility.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.5, “Integral normalized coordinate.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) be non-CM of analytic rank zero or one, and fix \\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the rational auxiliary tuple of Proposition 6, with\n  \\[\n  R=\\mathbb Z_p[[t,u_1,\\ldots,u_k]],\\qquad M_R=T_pE\\otimes R(\\chi).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the limiting unrestricted global complex, allowing moving primes, and \\(z\\in H^1(G)\\) the smoothed square class of Proposition 8 with \\(A=1\\) and symbol evaluation one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) contain \\(p,\\infty\\), and all bad primes. For fixed \\(q\\in S_f\\setminus\\{p\\}\\), put\n  \\[\n  D_q=\\det\\!\\left(1-\\frac{\\chi(\\operatorname{Frob}_q)\\rho_T(\\operatorname{Frob}_q)}q\n  \\,\\middle|\\,V_{I_q}\\right).\n  \\]\n  Let \\(P_q\\) be the inverse local \\(L\\)-polynomial in the period-formula orientation. Then\n  \\[\n  D_q(0)=P_q(0)=L_q(E,1)^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\) be the smoothing series from Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The generic cohomology of \\(G\\) is one degree-one line and \\(z\\neq0\\) on it. If \\(U_0\\) is the coordinate of \\(z\\) in the inverse determinant, then\n\\[\nU=\\frac{U_0}{\\Delta}\\prod_{q\\in S_f\\setminus\\{p\\}}\\frac{D_q}{P_q}\n\\in R\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\((p)\\), Proposition 6 makes \\(G\\) a free degree-one line, and all normalizing factors are units there. Generic nonvanishing follows from Rohrlich’s finite-support nonvanishing at sufficiently ramified even cyclotomic characters and Proposition 8’s period formula. Lemma 9.1 gives the single generic line. Removing tame variables adds acyclic singular factors and nonzero norm constants \\(2-\\operatorname{Tr}(g_j)\\), so nonvanishing also holds in the full diagram.</p><p>Lemma 9.1 treats every horizontal prime outside the fixed normalization factors. To remove those factors, express \\(U=g_0/f_0\\) by integral pivot formulas and retain integral stage numerators and denominators. Specialize tame variables to finite nontrivial characters avoiding zero denominators and prohibited \\(p\\)-Frobenius values.</p><p>At an actual stage and horizontal prime of \\(\\Lambda_\\eta\\), choose an unfilled rectangular class \\(z'\\), with nonzero symbol evaluation and smoothing \\(\\Delta'\\) nonzero at that prime. CRT choices tending to one \\(p\\)-adically, with a prescribed nontrivial active tame value, supply such smoothings. This class omits only \\(p\\) and ramified active conductors and has primitive old Euler factors.</p><p>Lemma 9.1 gives its determinant integrality with unramified old conditions. Passing to full old conditions changes valuation by \\(-\\sum_{q\\neq p}v(D_{q,\\eta})\\). In the common generic line, the square-to-rectangular ratio, apart from a nonzero constant symbol ratio, is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta\\prod_{q\\in S_f\\setminus\\{p\\}}P_q}{\\Delta'}.\n\\]\nProposition 8 verifies this at infinitely many nonvanishing high cyclotomic characters; bounded preparation gives the identity. Consequently every tested stage quotient has no horizontal pole, although no uniform stage-quotient denominator is asserted.</p><p>Remaining poles divide factors regular in \\(t\\). Divide the numerator by a distinguished representative to the needed multiplicity. Proposition 5’s pole persistence makes every permitted finite tame specialization of the remainder zero, including multiplicities. Finite-character detection kills the remainder in \\(R\\). There are no height-one poles; factoriality gives \\(U\\in R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.6, “Line comparison for a smoothed system.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use any of the following settings:", "statement": "<p>Use any of the following settings:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A height-one localization of \\(R\\) avoiding \\(p\\Delta\\prod_{q\\neq p}D_qP_q\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The generic field of the fixed-support cyclotomic ring, after removing all artificial variables.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. An actual stage with every tame variable specialized to a nontrivial finite character \\(\\eta_j\\), retaining \\(t\\), over \\(\\Lambda_\\eta=\\mathbb Z_p[\\eta][[t]]\\), generically or at a height-one prime not above \\(p\\); impose unramified old non-\\(p\\) conditions and choose \\(\\eta\\) to kill dual invariants at \\(p\\).</p><p>If the chosen square or rectangular system class \\(z_{\\mathrm{sys}}\\) is generically nonzero, the global complex has a single generic degree-one line, and at every indicated DVR,\n\\[\nd(G_{\\mathrm{test}},z_{\\mathrm{sys}})\\geq0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fixed non-\\(p\\) dual invariants vanish outside the excluded \\(D_q\\). At a moving place inertia forces \\(u_j=0\\), while on that divisor the chosen limiting Frobenius has no eigenvalue one. At \\(p\\), the true inertia equation is regular in \\(t\\), and a Frobenius lift trivial on the true quotient gives a nonzero equation independent of \\(t\\); the exponent condition prevents a common height-one component. The other settings have the corresponding cyclotomic or imposed invariant vanishing.</p><p>In setting 3, unramified old conditions use constant inertia. Their singular quotients have nonzero determinants\n\\[\nD_{q,\\eta}=\n\\det\\!\\left(1-\\frac{\\chi_t(\\operatorname{Frob}_q)\\rho_{V\\otimes\\eta}(\\operatorname{Frob}_q)}q\n\\,\\middle|\\,(V\\otimes\\eta)_{I_q}\\right),\n\\]\nand zero \\(H^1\\) over the DVR, even if the residue determinant vanishes. They retain \\(H^0\\), omit \\(H^2\\), and are exactly complementary through residue cup nullhomotopies.</p><p>Non-CM open image and Proposition 3’s evaluation argument give global invariant vanishing and detection on a joint kernel retaining a deep \\(\\operatorname{SL}_2\\). Poitou–Tate gives amplitude \\([1,2]\\) and \\(h=1+h^*\\).</p><p>Choose fresh primes tending to one whose Tate Frobenius tends to\n\\[\n\\tau=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;e\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad e\\neq0.\n\\]\nFinite and singular local coordinates are lines \\(T/(\\tau-1)T\\) and \\(\\ker(\\tau-1)\\), with perfect mixed pairing; \\(e\\) is a unit at the horizontal DVR. Joint evaluations make a nonzero primal and dual class nonzero on the finite line.</p><p>At each stage, fix the cyclotomic base first, perturb to make \\(v_p\\det(1-\\operatorname{Frob}_\\ell)\\) large but finite, and choose \\(A_i\\) with\n\\[\nA_i-v_p\\det(1-\\operatorname{Frob}_\\ell)\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n\\ell\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{A_i}}.\n\\]\nPrescribe mutual splitting with earlier derivative fields and radicals. Apply\n\\[\nD_I^{\\mathrm{der}}=\\prod_{\\ell\\in I}\\sum_{j=1}^{p^{A_i}-1}j\\sigma_\\ell^j.\n\\]\nA fixed \\(p\\)-power \\(L_*\\) clears uniformly bounded torsion and descent errors. The identity\n\\[\n(\\sigma_\\ell-1)D_\\ell^{\\mathrm{der}}=p^{A_i}-N_\\ell\n\\]\ngives descended integral classes \\(Z_I\\), with \\(Z_\\varnothing=L_*z_{\\mathrm{sys}}\\).</p><p>For lower and upper Frobenius cocycle values \\(x,x_\\ell\\), the norm relation gives\n\\[\n(F-1)y=mx_\\ell-\\ell^{-1}\\det(1-F)x,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\ny=m(F-1)^{-1}x_\\ell-\\ell^{-1}\\operatorname{adj}(F-1)x.\n\\]\nThe first term tends to zero by the chosen margin. Finite derivative values tend to zero, and the limiting singular value is the adjugate term. Thus\n\\[\nf(Z_I)=0,\\qquad\ns(Z_I)=\\operatorname{adj}(\\tau-1)f(Z_{I\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}}).\n\\]\nThe line comparison is a unit.</p><p>If generic \\(h&gt;1\\), the derived primal and old dual classes violate reciprocity at the chosen prime. Hence generic \\(h=1\\). On a larger residual fiber, apply Proposition 4’s line switch to a primitive generic-line reduction and a nonzero dual class. Repeated switches reach dimension one and preserve determinant valuation; the terminal class is integral. The fixed \\(L_*\\) is a horizontal unit.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 5.7, “Exact logarithmic comparison.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p&gt;2\\), a non-CM \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(a(E)=1\\), and fixed support \\(S\\), without moving primes.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_b\\) be the central smoothed square class and\n  \\[\n  d_0=\\Delta(0)\\prod_{q\\in S_f}P_q(1),\n  \\qquad P_q(1)=L_q(E,1)^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\in E(\\mathbb Q)\\) be nontorsion.</p><p class=\"li\">The vanishing dual exponential places \\(z_b\\) in the rational compact Selmer line, identified with \\(E(\\mathbb Q)\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{\\log_{\\omega_E}z_b}{(\\log_{\\omega_E}P)^2}\n=\n\\pm d_0\\frac{L'(E,1)}{\\Omega_0H(P)}.\n\\]\nThe right-hand quotient is rational and interpreted in \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\); the height is exactly \\(H\\) from Theorem 1.</p>", "proof": "<p>The twist over \\(K_b\\) has analytic rank zero, so\n\\[\nE(K_b)\\otimes\\mathbb Q=E(\\mathbb Q)\\otimes\\mathbb Q.\n\\]\nWrite \\(Q_X=t_PP\\) modulo torsion. Proposition 2’s Gross–Zagier normalization gives\n\\[\nC_0L'(f,1)L(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},1)\n=\n\\frac{2t_P^2H(P)}{\\delta_\\varphi}.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(t_P\\neq0\\). Trace compatibility gives\n\\[\nh_\\lambda^{K_b}(Q_X,Q_X)=2t_P^2h_\\lambda^{\\mathbb Q}(P,P).\n\\]\nSubstitute these identities into Lemma 10.3 and compare with Lemma 10.2. The nonzero factors \\(2-a_*\\), \\(h_\\lambda^{\\mathbb Q}(P,P)\\), and \\(\\log_{\\omega_E}P\\) cancel. This gives the exact logarithmic identity, up to the common orientation sign.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.8, “A nonzero height direction.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(K_b=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-D})\\) with all fixed primes split and \\(L(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},1)\\neq0\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(K_b=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-D})\\) with all fixed primes split and \\(L(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},1)\\neq0\\). There are primes \\(r_i\\), tending to one \\(p\\)-adically and split in the Hilbert class field of \\(K_b\\), and surjections\n\\[\n\\lambda_i:(\\mathbb Z/r_i\\mathbb Z)^\\times\\to\\mathbb Z/p^{m_i}\\mathbb Z,\\qquad m_i\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nkilling the prescribed fixed finite data, defining bilinear tame heights with rational limits such that\n\\[\nh_\\lambda^{K_b}(P_1,P_2)=2h_\\lambda^{\\mathbb Q}(P_1,P_2)\n\\quad(P_1,P_2\\in E(\\mathbb Q)),\n\\qquad\nh_\\lambda^{\\mathbb Q}(P,P)\\neq0.\n\\]\nThey are compatible with trace in the second argument.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the Poincaré biextension \\(G_y\\) for \\(y=\\operatorname{pol}(P)\\) and a rational lift \\(B_P\\). A Tate-module splitting has upper-right Kummer block \\(\\beta\\); writing the lift cocycle as \\((k,x)\\) gives \\(dk=-\\beta\\cup x\\).</p><p>Adjoin the fixed splitting field, cyclotomic tower, and radicals of the killed primes. Bounded derived length leaves a deep special-linear Tate image, and Kummer evaluations \\(x\\) still span the plane on the further Tate kernel. Choose \\(g\\), with determinant-one Tate action of trace \\(a_*\\neq\\pm2\\) and nontorsion eigenvalues, for which\n\\[\nk(g)-\\beta(g)g_T(g_T-1)^{-1}x(g)\\neq0.\n\\]\nExistence follows by multiplying by a commutator whose \\(k\\)-value is\n\\[\n\\pm2e(x(h_1),x(h_2))\\neq0.\n\\]\nChebotarev gives \\(r_i,\\lambda_i\\) realizing these data.</p><p>Over positive \\(r_i\\)-conductor ring-class fields, the normed character is unramified even above \\(r_i\\): the split class sequence leaves residue norm subgroup \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\), killed by the even character. Summing local character valuations of rigidified biextension lifts gives bilinearity and trace compatibility by reciprocity and the two biextension laws.</p><p>Since \\(\\#E(\\mathbb F_{r_i})\\to2-a_*\\neq0\\), one fixed \\(p^b\\) removes finite \\(p\\)-primary reduction parts. Divide the first argument by \\(p^{m_i}\\) in the uniquely \\(p\\)-divisible local factor and apply the character to the ratio of biextension lifts. Choices differ by \\(p^{m_i}\\)-th powers. Passing to limits and dividing by \\(p^b\\) gives the downstairs height.</p><p>All fixed-place contributions vanish for \\(B_P\\). With \\(w_i=(g_{i,T}-1)^{-1}x(g_i)\\), the remaining fiber cocycle is \\(p^b(k-\\beta\\cup w_i)\\). Its limiting Frobenius value is the displayed nonzero scalar. Trace over \\(K_b/\\mathbb Q\\) contributes degree two.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.9, “Cup comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the sequence of Lemma 10.1 and the square classes with the additional prime \\(r_i\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the sequence of Lemma 10.1 and the square classes with the additional prime \\(r_i\\). Let \\(Z_i(u)\\) be their group polynomial of dual-exponential coordinates, and \\(Z'\\) the linear coefficient of its bounded-series limit. Then\n\\[\nZ'\\log_{\\omega_E}P\n=\n\\pm(2-a_*)\\frac{\\log_{\\omega_E}z_b}{\\log_{\\omega_E}P}\nh_\\lambda^{\\mathbb Q}(P,P).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The characters kill \\(p\\), so all local coefficients pass through one fixed dual-exponential map, giving bounded denominators. Also \\(Z_i(0)=0\\), and at nontrivial characters\n\\[\nZ_i=\\pm d_0L(f,\\chi_b,1)/\\Omega_0.\n\\]</p><p>Write a cocycle modulo \\((p^{m_i},u^2)\\) as \\(z_0+uz_1\\), with \\(dz_1=-\\lambda_i\\cup z_0\\). The norm relation, after one fixed torsion clearing, gives\n\\[\nz_0=\\alpha_i\\beta,\\qquad\n\\alpha_i=\n\\pm\\left(1-\\frac{a_f(r_i)}{r_i}+\\frac1{r_i}\\right)\n\\frac{\\log_{\\omega_E}z_b}{\\log_{\\omega_E}P}.\n\\]\nThe scalar cochain \\(z_1\\cup x+\\alpha_i\\lambda_i\\cup k\\) is closed. Its \\(p\\)-invariant tends to \\(Z'\\log_{\\omega_E}P\\). Other fixed contributions vanish. At \\(r_i\\), the correction \\(dw_i=x\\) gives the scalar-ratio invariant \\(\\pm\\alpha_i h_i^{\\mathbb Q}(P,P)\\). The invariant sum is zero; the Euler factor tends to \\(2-a_*\\), giving the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.10, “Spectral-height identity.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\varphi:X_0(N)\\to E\\) have degree \\(\\delta_\\varphi\\), let \\(Q_X=\\varphi_*P_X\\) for the single oriented Hilbert-class trace over \\(K_b\\), and put \\( C_0=\\frac{\\sqrt D}{8\\pi^2(f,f)_N}. \\) Choose the twist period so …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\varphi:X_0(N)\\to E\\) have degree \\(\\delta_\\varphi\\), let \\(Q_X=\\varphi_*P_X\\) for the single oriented Hilbert-class trace over \\(K_b\\), and put\n\\[\nC_0=\\frac{\\sqrt D}{8\\pi^2(f,f)_N}.\n\\]\nChoose the twist period so that the rational period comparisons are interpreted in \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\). Then\n\\[\nC_0\\Omega_0L(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},1)\\frac{Z'}{d_0}\n=\n\\pm(2-a_*)\\frac{h_\\lambda^{K_b}(Q_X,Q_X)}{\\delta_\\varphi}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a fixed integral Hecke polynomial \\(\\mathcal A\\), away from \\(NDp\\), killing other level-\\(N\\) eigensystems and the relevant Eisenstein types, but not \\(f\\). Choose an inert good \\(\\ell\\) with \\(a_f(\\ell)\\neq0\\). Test indices prime to \\(ND\\) with odd \\(\\ell\\)-valuation.</p><p>Use the traced weight-one theta–Eisenstein kernel of [24, Section 6], with\n\\[\n\\theta(z)=\\frac12\\sum_C\\sum_{x\\in C}e(Nx\\,z/a_C),\n\\qquad \\rho(n)=\\sum_{d\\mid n}\\varepsilon_{K_b}(d).\n\\]\nIts coefficient at \\(nr^h\\) is\n\\[\nD_{i,h}(n;u)=\n\\frac12\n\\sum_{\\substack{C,\\ y\\in C,\\ b&gt;0\\\\a=Ny/a_C&gt;0\\\\a+Nb=nr^hD}}\n\\ \\sum_{\\substack{kl=b\\\\(k,l,D)=1}}\nW(k)\\chi(a/D)\\bar\\chi^2(Nk),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nW(k)=\\prod_{\\substack{q\\mid D\\\\q\\mid k}}(l/q)\n\\prod_{\\substack{q\\mid D\\\\q\\nmid k}}(-a_CN k/q).\n\\]\nPartial Fourier transforms, the Poisson scalar, and the primitive Gauss sum \\(i\\sqrt D\\) leave common scalar one, retaining the factor \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Differentiate the bounded group-polynomial limit. The identity \\(W(l)=-W(k)\\) removes divisor-independent terms and leaves weight \\(-2\\lambda_i(k)\\). Remove the \\(r\\)-unit mask to define \\(B_h\\), and put \\(C_h=B_h-B_{h-2}\\). Stripping common \\(r\\)-powers gives\n\\[\nC_{h+1}-2C_h+C_{h-1}=D'_{h+1}+2D'_h+D'_{h-1}.\n\\]\nThe multiplicities \\(1,4,8,12,\\ldots\\) have second differences \\(1,2,1\\); primitive-index-zero terms are affine.</p><p>At level \\(Nr^2\\), use \\(f_j=r^jf(r^jz)\\). The Fricke and unfolding identities are\n\\[\n\\frac{(I_\\chi,f_2)_{Nr^2}}{(f,f)_{Nr^2}}\n=\n\\frac{C_0}{r+1}\\frac{\\tau(\\chi)}{\\tau(\\bar\\chi)}\nL(f,\\bar\\chi,1)L(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},\\bar\\chi,1),\n\\qquad\n(I_\\chi,f_1)_{Nr^2}=0.\n\\]\nThe factors arise from the kernel, Fricke, Mellin, and level index \\(r(r+1)\\). The operator \\(U_r^h(U_r^2-1)\\) kills conductor-\\(r\\), conductor-\\(r^2\\), and ramified Eisenstein blocks; \\(\\mathcal A\\) kills the other old blocks.</p><p>For \\(a=a_f(r)\\), the normalized Gram entries satisfy\n\\[\ng_0=1,\\qquad g_1=\\frac a{r+1},\\qquad rg_2=ag_1-1.\n\\]\nThe coefficient on the \\(f_2\\) Gram row is\n\\[\ns_h=\\frac{r^2a_f(r^{h-2})-g_1r\\,a_f(r^{h-1})}{1-g_1^2}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(\\gamma_h=\\lim_i(a_f(r_i^h)-a_f(r_i^{h-2}))\\), the recurrence gives\n\\[\n\\lim_i\\frac{s_h}{r_i+1}\n=\n\\frac{\\gamma_h}{(a_*-2)(a_*+2)}.\n\\]</p><p>The Gauss ratio has bounded integral group coefficients and limiting augmentation one. The companion modular-symbol factor has limiting normalized augmentation\n\\[\n(2-a_*)L(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},1)/\\Omega_{\\mathrm{tw}}.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\((1+u)^2-1\\) to remove excluded character values, pass to bounded limits, and cancel. Since \\(Z_i(0)=0\\), only this companion augmentation contributes to the derivative. Thus the holomorphic coefficient difference is\n\\[\n\\pm \\mathcal A_fa_f(\\ell)\n\\frac{\\gamma_{h+2}-\\gamma_h}{(a_*-2)(a_*+2)}\n\\,C_0\\Omega_0\\frac{Z'}{d_0}(2-a_*)\nL(f\\otimes\\varepsilon_{K_b},1).\n\\]</p><p>The geometric coefficient identity identifies the weighted divisor sum with intersections of the oriented ordered Hilbert-class sums, based at \\(\\infty\\) and \\(0\\). Odd inert \\(\\ell\\)-valuation makes generic supports disjoint. Fixed-prime character contributions vanish. The cyclic-path difference removes continuing maximal-order paths; remaining orbits trace from positive-\\(r\\)-conductor ring-class fields. Their \\(r\\)-intersections vanish because ordinary CM reduction gives linear or conjugate-semilinear maps with even \\(\\ell\\)-valuation of norm degree.</p><p>Lemma 10.1 converts the intersections into full trace heights. Applying \\(\\mathcal A\\) inserts \\(\\varphi^*\\varphi_*/\\delta_\\varphi\\). The geometric second difference is\n\\[\n\\pm\\mathcal A_fa_f(\\ell)(a_*-2)(\\gamma_{h+2}-\\gamma_h)\n\\frac{h_\\lambda^{K_b}(Q_X,Q_X)}{\\delta_\\varphi}.\n\\]\nThe holomorphic coefficients \\(1,2,1\\) insert \\(a_*+2\\). Cancel common factors; \\(\\gamma_{h+2}-\\gamma_h\\neq0\\) for some large \\(h\\) because limiting eigenvalues are distinct and not roots of unity. The remaining scalar is \\(-1\\), giving the stated identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 5.11, “Central coordinate.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(p>2\\), let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) be non-CM with \\(a(E)\\leq1\\), and let \\(U\\in R\\) be Proposition 9’s normalized determinant coordinate.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(p&gt;2\\), let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) be non-CM with \\(a(E)\\leq1\\), and let \\(U\\in R\\) be Proposition 9’s normalized determinant coordinate. Then\n\\[\nX_p(E)=v_p(U(0))\\geq0.\n\\]\nThis holds for all reduction types and residual representations.</p>", "proof": "<p>At an actual central stage, let \\(S'=S\\cup\\{r_1,\\ldots,r_k\\}\\), and put\n\\[\nO^j=H^j(G_{\\mathbb Q,S'},T),\\quad\ns_0=v_p(\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)),\\quad\n\\tau_g=v_p(\\#E(\\mathbb Q)_{\\mathrm{tors}}),\\quad\n\\tau_v=v_p(\\#E(\\mathbb Q_v)[p^\\infty]).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\log_{\\omega_E}E(\\mathbb Q_p)=p^l\\mathbb Z_p\\). The singular quotient \\(J_p\\) is a free line, dual to local compact points modulo torsion. Integral Poitou–Tate gives\n\\[\nO^1\\to J_p\\to\\operatorname{Sel}_{p^\\infty}(E/\\mathbb Q)^\\vee\n\\to O^2\\to\\bigoplus_{v\\in S_f'}H^2(\\mathbb Q_v,T)\n\\to E(\\mathbb Q)[p^\\infty]^\\vee\\to0.\n\\]\nHere \\(O^0=0\\), \\(\\operatorname{rank}O^1=1\\), and \\(\\operatorname{length}O^1_{\\mathrm{tors}}=\\tau_g\\).</p><p>In rank zero, if \\(j=\\operatorname{length}(J_p/\\operatorname{im}O^1)\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}O^2=s_0-j+\\sum_v\\tau_v-\\tau_g.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\exp^*z_i^\\circ=\\alpha_i\\omega_E\\), local duality gives raw determinant valuation\n\\[\nv_p(\\alpha_i)+l+2\\tau_g-s_0-\\sum_v\\tau_v.\n\\]</p><p>In rank one, choose \\(P\\) generating the full free group. The cokernel of \\(J_p\\) in the dual global point line has length \\(v_p(\\log_{\\omega_E}P)-l\\), so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}O^2\n=\ns_0+v_p(\\log_{\\omega_E}P)-l+\\sum_v\\tau_v-\\tau_g.\n\\]\nThe raw valuation is\n\\[\nv_p\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log_{\\omega_E}z_i^\\circ}\n{(\\log_{\\omega_E}P)^2}\\right)\n+l+2\\tau_g-s_0-\\sum_v\\tau_v.\n\\]</p><p>Insert Proposition 8’s period formula in rank zero and Proposition 10 in rank one. Moving good norms restore exactly their omitted Euler factors. Proposition 4’s Haar identities cancel every omitted local factor. The result is\n\\[\nX_p(E)+v_p(\\Delta(0)).\n\\]\nThe full generator gives \\(H(P)=\\operatorname{Reg}_E\\), and \\(\\Omega_E/\\Omega_0\\) is a \\(p\\)-unit.</p><p>Moving local torsion lengths are bounded because limiting traces differ from two; the displayed formulas bound the remaining torsion. Proposition 5’s central-pivot observation therefore passes the raw valuation to the limit. Each \\(D_q/P_q\\) equals one centrally, and division by \\(\\Delta(0)\\) gives \\(v_p(U(0))=X_p(E)\\). Proposition 9’s integrality proves nonnegativity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Analytic CM measures and exact specialization.", "statement": "<p>Analytic CM measures and exact specialization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p&gt;2\\), the imaginary fields of Proposition 1, and an imaginary auxiliary tuple.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\varphi:X_0(N)\\to E\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\varphi^*\\omega_E=c_Ef\\,dq_{\\mathrm{Tate}}/q_{\\mathrm{Tate}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S^\\circ\\) be the fixed rational non-\\(p\\) support for \\(E/K\\), all split in \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each prime \\(q\\), put\n  \\[\n  P_q(Z)=1-\\frac{a_q}qZ+\\frac{\\epsilon_q}qZ^2,\n  \\qquad\n  \\epsilon_q=\n  \\begin{cases}1&amp;q\\nmid N,\\\\0&amp;q\\mid N.\\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">“Ordinary” refers to the modular source, not a reduction hypothesis on the target \\(E\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are bounded integral CM series \\(b_w^+,b_w^-,b_w^0\\) and \\(B_w=b_w^+b_w^-\\), initially over \\(\\mathcal V^*[[t,\\mathbf u]]\\), satisfying Lemmas 12.1–12.4. Their principal ideals descend to \\(R=\\mathbb Z_p[[t,\\mathbf u]]\\), with only integral-unit changes of generators.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 12.1 constructs the integral disk measures. Lemma 12.2 assembles them into bounded CM series with exact finite-character and central formulas. Lemma 12.3 gives their integral conductor-removal laws, and Lemma 12.4 permits all subsequent ideal comparisons over \\(R\\). These arguments do not impose ordinary reduction on \\(E\\) and include CM curves.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, “Integral logarithmic primitive.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On the canonical ordinary locus, let \\(V_q\\) be quotient pullback normalized by \\(q_{\\mathrm{Tate}}\\mapsto q_{\\mathrm{Tate}}^q\\), and let \\(d_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\) be ordinary modular differentiation.", "statement": "<p>On the canonical ordinary locus, let \\(V_q\\) be quotient pullback normalized by \\(q_{\\mathrm{Tate}}\\mapsto q_{\\mathrm{Tate}}^q\\), and let \\(d_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\) be ordinary modular differentiation. Define\n\\[\nf^{[p]}=(1-a_pV_p+p\\epsilon_pV_p^2)f,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_0=\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}d_{\\mathrm{mod}}^{(p-1)p^j-1}f^{[p]},\n\\qquad\nF=\\prod_{q\\in S^\\circ}P_q(V_q)F_0.\n\\]\nThese are integral weight-zero functions with\n\\[\nd_{\\mathrm{mod}}F_0=f^{[p]},\\qquad\nF=\\sum_{\\substack{n\\geq1\\\\(n,p\\prod_{q\\in S^\\circ}q)=1}}\n\\frac{a_n}{n}q_{\\mathrm{Tate}}^n.\n\\]\nOn a Serre–Tate disk, \\(F\\) is the transform of a bounded integral measure supported on \\(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\). With\n\\[\nG(X)=c_E^{-1}\\log_{\\omega_E}\\varphi(X),\n\\]\none has exactly\n\\[\nF_0=P_p(V_p)G.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work on a fine prime-to-\\(p\\) level and its Igusa tower. The trivialized Kodaira–Spencer differential defines \\(d_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\). Integral cusp expansions detect reduction on every determinant component and every finite trivialization level.</p><p>The \\(p\\)-depletion has only unit indices. Uniform convergence\n\\[\nn^{(p-1)p^j-1}\\to n^{-1}\n\\]\nand convergence of weights to zero give integral convergence on the tower. Full \\(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\)-invariance descends \\(F_0\\) to the ordinary curve. Hecke recursions give the depleted expansion.</p><p>Mahler duality gives the measure. Trace along the canonical quotient, \\(z\\mapsto z^p\\), gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\zeta^p=1}F(\\zeta z)=0.\n\\]\nOn transforms this kills the restriction to \\(p\\mathbb Z_p\\); uniqueness and torsion-freeness give unit support.</p><p>The modular map extends on the smooth canonical ordinary charts by the Néron property, including bad target reduction. Its logarithm is analytic on interior residue disks. The differentials of \\(F_0\\) and \\(P_p(V_p)G\\) agree, so their difference is constant. The \\(T_p\\) or \\(U_p\\) correspondence gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\zeta^p=1}G(\\zeta z)=a_pG(B)-\\epsilon_pG(V_pB).\n\\]\nCuspidal differences are torsion and vanish under logarithm. Thus \\(P_p(V_p)G\\) also has trace zero. All \\(p\\) sources lie in one disk, so \\(p\\) times the constant difference is zero in characteristic zero. The constant is zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Proposition 6.2, “Finite characters and the center.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At tame conductor \\(s\\), let \\(\\mu_{\\mathfrak a}\\) be the disk measures, \\(t_{\\mathfrak a}\\in\\Gamma\\) the Artin labels, and \\(\\delta_{\\mathfrak a}\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\) the frame exponents.", "statement": "<p>At tame conductor \\(s\\), let \\(\\mu_{\\mathfrak a}\\) be the disk measures, \\(t_{\\mathfrak a}\\in\\Gamma\\) the Artin labels, and \\(\\delta_{\\mathfrak a}\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\) the frame exponents. The local-unit map\n\\[\n\\eta_\\Gamma:\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\to\\Gamma\n\\]\nhas kernel \\(\\mu_{p-1}\\) and open image. Define\n\\[\nb_s(t,\\mathbf u)=\n\\sum_{\\mathfrak a\\in\\operatorname{Pic}(\\mathcal O_s)}\n[t_{\\mathfrak a}-\\eta_\\Gamma(\\delta_{\\mathfrak a})]\\psi_s(\\mathfrak a)\n\\int_{\\mathbb Z_p^\\times}[-\\eta_\\Gamma(x)]\\,d\\mu_{\\mathfrak a}(x).\n\\]</p><p>For high finite \\(\\theta\\), let \\(\\theta_l=\\theta\\circ\\eta_\\Gamma\\) have primitive conductor \\(p^m\\), put \\(\\chi=\\theta\\psi_s\\), and let \\(X\\) have parameter \\(\\zeta_{p^m}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_{j\\bmod p^m}\\theta_l(j)\\zeta_{p^m}^j\\right)b_s(\\theta,\\mathbf u)\n=\n\\left(\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(H_{p^ms}/K)}\n\\chi(\\rho)G(X^\\rho)\\right)\n\\prod_{q\\in S^\\circ}P_q(\\chi(\\sigma_q)).\n\\]\nHere \\(\\sigma_q\\) is inverse oriented \\(V_q\\)-translation.</p><p>For \\(b_w^0\\) formed without non-\\(p\\) depletion,\n\\[\nB_w=\\text{unit}\\cdot(b_w^0)^2D,\n\\quad\nD=\\prod_{q\\in S^\\circ}\nP_q(\\chi(\\operatorname{Frob}_{v_q}))\nP_q(\\chi(\\operatorname{Frob}_{v_q})^{-1}).\n\\]\nAt \\(s=1\\),\n\\[\nB_w^{s=1}(0)=\n\\pm c_E^{-2}\\log_{\\omega_E}(P_K)^2P_p(1)^2\n\\prod_{q\\in S^\\circ}P_q(1)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Endomorphism lifting identifies primitive Serre–Tate roots with conductor-\\(p^ms\\) CM points. The order-class kernel acts simply transitively on these roots. Finite Fourier summation gives the Gauss factor. Frame transport changes exponents by \\(\\delta_{\\mathfrak a}\\), and the correcting group-like term gives exactly the Artin character weight.</p><p>Apply Lemma 12.1. High primitive characters kill the \\(V_p,V_p^2\\) terms through lower-conductor orbits; the other quotients give oriented translations.</p><p>Non-\\(p\\) Atkin–Lehner operations reverse orientations, acting on raw logarithmic sums by signs and translation characters, since base-cusp differences are torsion. Test high characters and use bounded uniqueness to obtain the squared undepleted factor and \\(D\\).</p><p>At augmentation, integration evaluates disk centers. The \\(p\\)-Euler factor remains because \\(V_p\\) translates conductor-one centers. Each sum is once over the class group, giving the single Hilbert-class trace and the stated center.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, “Removal of tame conductor variables.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose each new base disk by a descending isogeny.", "statement": "<p>Choose each new base disk by a descending isogeny. Setting its tame variable to zero multiplies each single analytic series by\n\\[\na_r-\\chi_{\\mathrm{old}}(\\operatorname{Frob}_{v_r})\n-\\chi_{\\mathrm{old}}(\\operatorname{Frob}_{v_r})^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe strict determinant \\(L_w\\) is multiplied by the square of this factor up to an integral unit. These identities hold successively for any subset of artificial variables, including their complete removal.</p>", "proof": "<p>The good Hecke correspondence minus its two horizontal split translations is the conductor-\\(rs\\) to conductor-\\(s\\) trace, giving the analytic factor. Polarization transports frame exponents by \\(r_i^{\\pm1}\\to1\\), so the identity survives at high true characters and bounded group-ring limits without character projectors.</p><p>Unramified inflation restores the full pair at the removed prime. Its two singular Euler determinants have product the square of the displayed factor, up to an integral unit, since \\(r_i\\to1\\). This is an integral determinant-triangle identity. The factor need not be a central unit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.4", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, “Descent of the analytic ideals.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The principal ideals of \\(b_w^+,b_w^-,b_w^0,B_w\\) descend to \\(R\\).", "statement": "<p>The principal ideals of \\(b_w^+,b_w^-,b_w^0,B_w\\) descend to \\(R\\). Each actual series differs from an \\(R\\)-generator by an integral unit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Extend arithmetic unramified Frobenius at \\(w\\) to fix \\(p\\)-power roots of unity. The interpolation formula identifies its action on a raw CM sum with translation, so\n\\[\n\\phi(b)=\\kappa b\n\\]\nfor group-like \\(\\kappa\\) of augmentation one. On \\(\\mathcal V^*\\), \\(\\phi\\) is Witt Frobenius.</p><p>Solve \\(\\phi(e)=\\kappa e\\), with constant coefficient one, recursively in total degree. Each step is \\(\\phi(a)-a=b_0\\), soluble modulo \\(p\\) over the algebraically closed residue field and then by successive lifting. Thus \\(b/e\\) is Witt-Frobenius fixed and has coefficients in \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\). Products give the result for \\(B_w\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 6.5, “Vertical analytic primitivity.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For either \\(p\\)-adic place and every imaginary auxiliary tuple under consideration, \\( p\\nmid B_w. \\)", "statement": "<p>For either \\(p\\)-adic place and every imaginary auxiliary tuple under consideration,\n\\[\np\\nmid B_w.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First prove tame-stalk independence. Let \\(x\\) be an ordinary maximal-order CM point with endomorphism ring \\(\\mathcal O_K\\), and let \\(\\mathscr R\\) be its algebraic local ring on the prime-to-\\(p\\) modular tower, not its completion. For \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_h\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\), consider\n\\[\n\\Phi:\\mathscr R^{\\otimes h}\\to\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p[[z-1]],\n\\qquad\nf_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_h\\mapsto\\prod_if_i(z^{a_i}).\n\\]\nAssume pairwise ratios are inequivalent under\n\\[\n\\{\\alpha/\\bar\\alpha:\\alpha\\in K^\\times,\\ \\alpha\\text{ a unit above }p\\}.\n\\]\nSuppose \\(\\ker\\Phi\\neq0\\), with \\(h\\) minimal. Its irreducible relation locus projects dominantly to each proper product. The dense common CM stabilizer and the rigidity/normalization argument of [13] give a finite tame model whose normalized branch is a formal subtorus of dimension \\(h-1\\).</p><p>The canonical-coordinate relation sheaf on the normalization has, on a dense smooth open, a smooth cocharacter subsheaf \\(N\\) of rank \\(h-1\\) inside the direct sum of the \\(h\\) Serre–Tate lines. Every coordinate projection is nonzero, by dominance.</p><p>If all generic elliptic factors were pairwise nonisogenous, semisimplicity, the isogeny theorem, and Lie-algebra Goursat give joint derived monodromy \\(\\operatorname{SL}_2^h\\). A diagonal block between two factors would intertwine standard representations; equal determinants and connected center make the intertwiner equivariant, forcing an isogeny. Chebotarev therefore supplies a closed point with pairwise distinct squared traces and hence distinct squared unit roots \\(\\alpha_i^2\\). Frobenius acts on cocharacter lines by \\(\\alpha_i^{-2}\\). Its stable subspace \\(N\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p\\) is a sum of coordinate axes; nonzero projection to every coordinate forces rank \\(h\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Thus two generic factors are isogenous. Extend their isogeny over a normal finite cover and specialize at the CM branch. Serre–Tate functoriality gives\n\\[\na_i/a_j=\\alpha/\\bar\\alpha\n\\]\nup to inversion. Equal \\(p\\)-valuations allow rational scaling to make \\(\\alpha\\) prime to \\(p\\), contradicting inequivalence. Hence \\(\\Phi\\) is injective.</p><p>Now set tame conductor \\(s=1\\). Proposition 12’s weight-zero descent places reduced germs in these algebraic tame stalks. If the conductor-one series vanished modulo \\(p\\), folding its measure through \\(\\eta_\\Gamma\\) would give\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathfrak a}\\sum_{\\zeta\\in\\mu_{p-1}}\nF_{\\mathfrak a}(z^{\\zeta\\delta_{\\mathfrak a}/\\lambda_{\\mathfrak a}})=0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\eta_\\Gamma(\\lambda_{\\mathfrak a})=t_{\\mathfrak a}\\) for the contributing classes. Pull ideal isogenies to a common tame level and combine equivalent slots.</p><p>The slot equivalent to one consists of ramified ideal products. Indeed, if \\(\\mathfrak a^h=(x)\\), then\n\\[\n\\lambda_{\\mathfrak a}^h=(x/\\bar x)\\xi\n\\]\nfor torsion \\(\\xi\\); equivalence of \\(\\zeta/\\lambda_{\\mathfrak a}\\) with \\(\\alpha/\\bar\\alpha\\) and torsion-free ideal valuations makes a principal scaling of \\(\\mathfrak a\\) conjugation invariant. These are products of ramified primes. Conversely their squares are rational principal, so their true labels vanish. Only \\(\\zeta=\\pm1\\) occurs, and complementary ramified subsets represent the classes twice, with conjugate ratio \\(-1\\).</p><p>At a common Tate cusp with all those kernels multiplicative, the identity translate has first positive coefficient one. Every other ramified product has larger degree and begins at a larger index. The combined slot is nonconstant, contradicting stalk independence. Thus each orientation series at \\(s=1\\) is nonzero modulo \\(p\\).</p><p>Finally restore moving conductors using Proposition 12. Each conductor factor has nonzero true exponent and is a nonzero Laurent polynomial modulo \\(p\\). After setting new variables to zero, the product of nonzero reductions remains nonzero. Hence \\(p\\nmid B_w\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "High-character comparison and integral quotients.", "statement": "<p>High-character comparison and integral quotients.</p><p>Original labels: Proposition 6.6, “Divisibility at high finite characters,” and Corollary 6.9, “The integral comparison quotient.”</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a sufficiently high finite character \\(\\theta\\) of the true anticyclotomic group and put\n  \\[\n  A_\\theta=\\mathbb Z_p[\\theta][[\\mathbf u]][1/p],\n  \\qquad\n  \\widetilde A_\\theta=\\mathcal V^*[\\theta][[\\mathbf u]][1/p].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A'_\\theta\\) be the finite faithfully flat coefficient extension containing the required Gauss coordinates and fixed ramified local normal resolvents.</p><p class=\"li\">Use descended analytic ideals for integral comparisons.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nB_w(\\theta,\\mathbf u)\\in L_w(\\theta,\\mathbf u)A'_\\theta.\n\\]\nFor the same global scalar character, the two nonzero quotients at \\(w,\\bar w\\) are associates.</p><p>Consequently,\n\\[\nU_w=B_w/L_w\\in R,\\qquad p\\nmid U_w.\n\\]\nRemoving any newly added tame variables specializes \\(U_w\\) to the old quotient times an integral unit, including removal of every artificial variable.</p>", "proof": "<p>At high \\(\\theta\\), moving local complexes are acyclic at every height-one test: inertia detects their own variables, and on \\(u_j=0\\) the high true-character value avoids elliptic eigenvalue exceptions. Replace fixed non-\\(p\\) full conditions by exact complementary unramified conditions; their removed singular product is \\(D\\) from Proposition 12. The primitive strict determinant is \\(L_w/D\\).</p><p>Let \\(Z\\) be the weighted CM Kummer class, with one fixed clearing multiple, and \\(p_0\\) its coordinate in the Kummer line at \\(w\\). Lemma 14.1 gives\n\\[\nB_w(\\theta,\\mathbf u)=\\text{unit in }A'_\\theta\\cdot p_0^2D.\n\\]\nHigh-character nonvanishing follows from Propositions 6 and 13 and bounded preparation. Proposition 4’s strict–finite comparison gives generic ranks one in degrees one and two for the all-Kummer complex \\(C_F\\), and\n\\[\nd(C_F,(Z,Z^\\vee))\n=\n2v_{\\mathfrak p}(p_0)-v_{\\mathfrak p}(L_w/D).\n\\]</p><p>If fiber \\(H^1\\) has dimension greater than one, choose two independent classes, including a primitive generic-line reduction. Evaluation detection on the joint Tate/ring-class kernel applies through the central homothety above. The opposite scalar twists are absolutely simple and nonisomorphic. Their joint evaluations span both copies, and evaluation on \\((\\tau\\eta_b)^2\\) gives\n\\[\n(a_1+Jb_1,a_2+Jb_2).\n\\]\nAn integer grid in characteristic zero avoids the rank-defect locus, giving rank-two finite evaluation. Chebotarev realizes it.</p><p>Apply Lemma 14.2 and Proposition 4’s square switch. Dimension drops by two, generic ranks and volume are preserved, and upper semicontinuity prevents dropping below one. At terminal dimension one the tensor is integral. Hence\n\\[\np_0^2/(L_w/D)\\in A'_\\theta,\n\\]\nproving high-character divisibility.</p><p>For \\(\\bar w\\), high-conductor canonical ascending CM edges give the same points and all-Kummer tensor up to non-\\(p\\) Atkin–Lehner and translation units. Quotient valuations agree, so the principal ideals are equal. Faithful flatness descends this equality.</p><p>Now Proposition 5’s integral character division applies because \\(L_w\\not\\equiv0\\pmod{(p,t)}\\). It gives \\(L_w\\mid B_w\\) in \\(R\\). Proposition 13 gives \\(p\\nmid U_w\\). Proposition 12’s conductor-removal law restores the same nonzero square factor in numerator and denominator; cancellation leaves an integral-unit specialization identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.7, “The two local Kummer lines.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After fixing high \\(\\theta\\), local degree-one cohomology at each \\(p\\)-place splits over \\(A_\\theta\\) into a free Kummer line and a free complementary line.", "statement": "<p>After fixing high \\(\\theta\\), local degree-one cohomology at each \\(p\\)-place splits over \\(A_\\theta\\) into a free Kummer line and a free complementary line. The conjugate Kummer conditions are exactly orthogonal.</p><p>After extension to \\(A'_\\theta\\), logarithm identifies the Kummer line with a free line and evaluates weighted CM point classes by Proposition 12’s interpolation formula, with \\(c_E\\) restored. This includes bad reduction at \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Separate the fixed totally ramified character extension \\(P_v/K_v\\) from the unramified \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-tower. The projected Shapiro ring is\n\\[\n\\Lambda_\\theta=\\mathbb Z_p[\\theta][[X]][1/p].\n\\]\nIts substitution into \\(A_\\theta\\) is\n\\[\n1+X\\longmapsto\n\\alpha_v\\prod_j(1+u_j)^{e_{vj}},\n\\]\nwith nonzero exponent vector. Divide out its common \\(p^a\\), complete the primitive vector to group-power coordinates, and obtain the monic distinguished equation\n\\[\n(1+y_1)^{p^a}=\\alpha_v^{-1}(1+X).\n\\]\nThus substitution is flat.</p><p>With constants depending on \\(\\theta\\), a sufficiently small additive lattice exponentiates. After a fixed semistable extension, components are bounded under unramified growth. A sufficiently deep nontrivial inertia difference sends identity-component points into the formal kernel; on the projected character line that difference is a nonzero constant. This bounds the logarithm lattice and torsion kernel uniformly through the unramified tower. Trace-dual bounds use the fixed different.</p><p>Integral Kummer and local duality give an exact compact inverse-limit sequence. Trace-compatible integral normal generators in the unramified tower, with total trace one, identify the additive inverse limit with a regular rank-one module; a fixed ramified rational normal basis and character projection give the same after inverting \\(p\\). Both Kummer and dual endpoints are free rank one. Flat substitution yields the split lines over \\(A_\\theta\\), and finite Kummer isotropy gives exact orthogonality.</p><p>Form unnormalized logarithmic group polynomials before substitution. Ramified resolvents are nonzero and unramified resolvents are units. Specialize bounded cyclic quotients, trace stage points to the corresponding local fields, and use finite-level logarithmic evaluation. Freeness detects the resulting class membership and coordinates, yielding the stated formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.8, “Inert derivative coordinates.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are fresh inert good prime sequences \\(\\ell\\to-1\\), with Tate Frobenius tending to the elliptic complex-conjugation involution \\(J\\), for which local degree-one cohomology splits into finite and transverse planes.", "statement": "<p>There are fresh inert good prime sequences \\(\\ell\\to-1\\), with Tate Frobenius tending to the elliptic complex-conjugation involution \\(J\\), for which local degree-one cohomology splits into finite and transverse planes. Their mixed pairing is \\(\\langle x,Jy\\rangle\\) up to a unit, and both pure planes are self-annihilating.</p><p>For any fixed finite sequence of switches, there are descended weighted Kummer classes \\(Z_I\\), using ring-class derivatives at \\(I\\), retaining all previous local conditions and one common point scale, such that\n\\[\nf_{\\mathrm{loc}}(Z_{I\\cup\\{\\ell\\}})=0,\\qquad\ns_{\\mathrm{loc}}(Z_{I\\cup\\{\\ell\\}})=Jf_{\\mathrm{loc}}(Z_I).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose Frobenius \\(\\gamma_i\\to\\tau\\), with \\(\\tau\\) complex conjugation. Then \\(a_f(\\ell)\\to0\\), and \\(\\gamma_i^2\\) acts trivially on ring-class fields by the dihedral relation. The limiting residue–inertia resolution gives free finite and transverse planes, perfect mixed pairing, and the indicated isotropy.</p><p>The relative ring-class order is \\(\\ell+1\\). Use\n\\[\nD_\\ell=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}j\\sigma_\\ell^j,\\qquad\n(\\sigma_\\ell-1)D_\\ell=(\\ell+1)-\\operatorname{Tr}_\\ell.\n\\]\nFor upper point \\(Y\\), lower point \\(X\\), the Hecke trace is \\(a_\\ell X\\) and supersingular reduction gives \\(\\widetilde Y=F_\\ell\\widetilde X\\). Choose growing precision dividing \\(\\ell+1,a_\\ell\\) and with \\(\\gamma_i^2=1\\) to that precision.</p><p>A fixed homothety \\(h\\) gives \\(h\\tau h\\tau\\), trivial on ring-class actions and nontrivial scalar on Tate coefficients, bounding all invariant torsion. Inflation–restriction descends derivatives after one fixed multiple. Lang’s theorem, formal-group successive quotients, and bounded components retain the old Kummer and unramified conditions.</p><p>Choose \\(p^aQ=X\\), \\(p^aW=D_\\ell Y\\). The crossed identity gives the upper transverse coordinate, after reduction, as\n\\[\n(a_\\ell-(\\ell+1)F_\\ell)\\widetilde Q\n=\n(F_\\ell-a_\\ell)(F_\\ell^2-1)\\widetilde Q.\n\\]\nThe lower finite coordinate is \\((F_\\ell^2-1)\\widetilde Q\\). Thus the limiting transverse comparison is \\(F_\\ell-a_\\ell\\to J\\). The derivative has an \\(F_\\ell^2\\)-fixed division point, giving zero upper finite coordinate. The calculation applies before weighted corestriction and with earlier derivative factors, so all switches share the common scale.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 7.9, “Vanishing of all constant terms.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(K,F,L\\), \\(p&gt;2\\), and the universal anticyclotomic scalar \\(\\chi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be the algebraic norm-one-torus character of infinity type one, pulled to \\(L/F\\). Narrow the weight branch so that difference powers of its \\(p\\)-avatar factor through the free group \\(\\Gamma\\), with nonzero logarithmic direction.</p><p class=\"li\">The compact theta source is \\(U(A)\\), \\(A=(L^2,1_2)\\). The target is \\(U(W)\\), with\n  \\[\n  W=B\\oplus H,\\quad B=(L^2,b_0\\sqrt D\\,1_2),\\quad H=Le\\oplus Lf,\n  \\]\n  of signatures \\((3,1)\\), and distinguished column order \\((e,B_1,B_2,f)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi\\) be the compact transfer of the real quadratic base change of \\(E\\); its standard base change to \\(L\\) is cuspidal and its local elliptic parameters are tempered.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_0\\) be an interior algebraic point supplied by Lemma 15.2, with \\(b_w^0(z_0)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At stage \\(i\\), use the finite tame groups and a growing true quotient \\(G_i\\), universal character \\(\\nu_i\\), and positive weight \\(m\\) on the fixed branch. Put \\(\\eta=\\mathcal C^m\\nu_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After one fixed integral clearing, weights \\(m_i\\) can be chosen, tending to zero \\(p\\)-adically on the branch and to infinity ordinarily, such that every constant Fourier–Jacobi coefficient of the theta section \\(\\Theta_{i,m_i}\\), evaluated at \\(z_0\\), tends uniformly to zero.</p><p>The assertion holds for the finitely many fixed variants used for nonconstant content and their integral prime-to-\\(p\\) translates. At each stage arbitrarily strong additional weight approximations may be imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>At zero Fourier–Jacobi index, hyperbolic-column monomials vanish; the surviving determinant monomial is a binary theta value. Its local split intertwiner is the central Godement–Jacquet map\n\\[\nJ_v(\\Phi,z)=\n\\int_{\\operatorname{GL}_2(F_v)}\n\\Phi({}^th)|\\det h|_v\\,\\pi_v^\\vee(h)\\eta_v^{-1}(\\det h)z\\,d^\\times h.\n\\]\nUse the equal-rank anisotropic binary Siegel–Weil/Rallis identity and two Waldspurger identities in the normalization of [23, Section 5]. Retain partial \\(L\\)-functions and raw exceptional integrals, without dividing by a global \\(L\\)-value.</p><p>At \\(p\\), the Mellin integral is exactly one. The binary and toric gamma factors match, including conductors, and cancel; remaining frame factors are units.</p><p>At a moving prime \\(q\\), let \\(D_q^f\\) be the inverse fake Euler polynomial. The raw toric factor is\n\\[\nH_q^f=\nD_q^f\\sum_{a\\in\\mathbb F_q^\\times}\\chi_q^{\\mathrm{Mell}}(a)\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}}(a/q)\n+(1-D_q^f)\\sum_{a\\in\\mathbb F_q^\\times}\\chi_q^{\\mathrm{Mell}}(a).\n\\]\nThe additive sum is a unit, with residual augmentation \\(-1\\); the plain unit sum is a norm-element multiple tending to zero at the test. Lemma 15.2 makes \\(D_q^f\\) have nonzero limiting value, so \\(H_q^f(z_0)\\) has bounded valuation. No inverse growing group order remains.</p><p>At fixed split primes, divide local matrix maps by their local Euler factors. Their coefficients are Laurent polynomials with fixed denominators; local base change and epsilon-factor induction cancel the toric Euler factors rationally before specialization. Thus no vanishing inverse Euler polynomial is inverted at \\(z_0\\). Nonsplit local scalars are trivial and fixed no-pole tests apply. Archimedean factorial ratios \\((m-1)!m!\\) cancel, as do the matching CM period weights.</p><p>For every constant coefficient \\(a_{i,m}\\), the bilinear algebraic comparison, cross-multiplied, is\n\\[\nA_{i,m}a_{i,m}^2\n=\nB_{i,m}P_{0,i}(m)^2P_{1,i}(m)^2.\n\\]\nThe multipliers have uniformly bounded group coefficients, and \\(A_{i,m}(z_0)\\) is nonzero with uniformly bounded valuation. Uniformity across cusps follows as follows. At moving primes, deficient-rank coefficients are integral norm-element multiples; containment-lattice sums have only fake Euler denominators cleared by \\(D_q^f,H_q^f\\). At \\(p\\), integral matrix support and determinant equivariance give a convergent matrix-zeta series for large positive \\(m\\), with factor \\(p^{(m-1)v_p(\\det h)}\\), fixed source volume, and no deep projector. Fixed-place tests have the Laurent bounds above.</p><p>Finite characters separate the characteristic-zero group algebra, so the cross-multiplied identities hold coefficientwise and can be evaluated to increasing precision at \\(z_0\\). Lemma 15.8 makes their right sides tend to zero. The bounded inverse valuation of \\(A_{i,m}(z_0)\\) gives uniform constant-term vanishing.</p><p>Choose each finite stage and depth first, then weight close enough to zero to pay its finite losses, then ordinarily large enough for convergence and holomorphy. Retain precision tending to infinity slowly. This allows all additional finite weight congruences required later.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “Auxiliary tests.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are two disjoint sets of extra split good control primes and corresponding fixed theta data, spherical elsewhere except at elliptic bad primes.", "statement": "<p>There are two disjoint sets of extra split good control primes and corresponding fixed theta data, spherical elsewhere except at elliptic bad primes. At unramified-twist Steinberg places use source Iwahori and target line-stabilizer parahoric tests; additive places allow fixed nonspherical tests.</p><p>For either set, there are \\(\\phi\\in\\pi^\\vee\\) and finite-order \\(d_0\\) on the stabilizer torus \\(T=U(Le_2)\\) with\n\\[\nI_T(\\phi,d_0)=\\int_{[T]}\\phi(s)d_0(s)\\,ds\\neq0.\n\\]\nCompatible unary characters \\(\\xi_{j,d}\\), of weights \\(d+c_j\\), \\(c_1+c_2=1\\), satisfy\n\\[\n\\xi_{1,d_1}\\xi_{2,d_2}=\\mathcal C^md_0\n\\quad(d_1+d_2+1=m).\n\\]\nTheir unit-position unary periods \\(T_j(d)\\) are separately nonzero at positive \\(d_{10},d_{20}\\), with \\(d_{10}+d_{20}+1\\) divisible by the required torsion orders and any prescribed fixed power of \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Ray-character constructions place extra conductors at arbitrarily deep new split places, remove finite unit obstructions, and extend over finite ray-class quotients. Make these choices twice with disjoint sets.</p><p>Matched spherical and split unary lattice tests have nonzero central local integrals. Translate a compact source vector at a free split place until its restriction to the torus is nonzero; strong approximation retains the other compact conditions. Fourier expansion on the compact torus quotient supplies \\(d_0\\). A fixed diagonal \\(p\\)-translation retains nonzero period and lower-unipotent invariance.</p><p>Apply [23, Lemma 6.2] with coherent matched unary lines, unramified nonsplit data, self-dual critical characters, a reduction prime greater than two split in \\(L\\), and a split degree-one variation prime. Inverse variation preserves the product of characters. Unit cuts at \\(p\\) and the congruence choice give the asserted degrees. Narrowing the analytic branches can be done before these nonvanishing choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Choice of a point.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathfrak p\\) be a horizontal divisor with \\(b_w^0\\in\\mathfrak p\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathfrak p\\) be a horizontal divisor with \\(b_w^0\\in\\mathfrak p\\). Fix a strict side for each of \\(V_pE\\) and \\(V_pE^{D'}\\), and assume both corresponding primitive strict groups vanish generically on \\(\\mathfrak p\\).</p><p>Then an interior point \\(z_0\\) on \\(\\mathfrak p\\), over one finite extension \\(k/\\mathbb Q_p\\), retains those vanishings, all required local invariant vanishings and moving-place acyclicity, and nonzero denominators for one auxiliary set of Lemma 15.1.</p><p>At moving primes, inverse fake Euler factors can be chosen nonzero; binary separation uses inertia when the tested unit character is ramified and Frobenius when it is unramified.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cohomological vanishings are open nonvanishing-minor conditions. Fixed good-place spanning and binary separation exceptions are zeros of finitely many nonzero Laurent polynomials in the local scalar parameter. Two distinct rational primes have independent exponent vectors, so they cannot both be identically exceptional on a height-one divisor. One of the disjoint auxiliary sets avoids the possible exceptional prime.</p><p>At a moving prime, the inverse fake Euler factor has degree at most two in a uniformizer value. Changing uniformizer by a unit does not alter the cleared comparison. A nontrivial tested unit character has at least three values because \\(p\\) is odd, so one avoids the two roots. If the character is trivial on the divisor, its own coordinate is zero and the prescribed trace gives nonzero Frobenius tests. In the ramified case a nontrivial unit value gives inertia separation.</p><p>Apply Proposition 5’s interior-point principle to the remaining finite nonzero conditions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, “Canonical levels.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At \\(p\\), use \\( \\Phi_p(Y)= \\varphi_p(Y_{[e,B_1]}) \\nu_{i,p}(\\det Y_{[e,B_1]}) 1_{M_{2,2}(\\mathbb Z_p)}(Y_{[B_2,f]}), \\qquad \\operatorname{supp}\\varphi_p\\subset\\operatorname{GL}_2(\\mathbb Z_p). \\) Let \\(J(s)\\) have …", "statement": "<p>At \\(p\\), use\n\\[\n\\Phi_p(Y)=\n\\varphi_p(Y_{[e,B_1]})\n\\nu_{i,p}(\\det Y_{[e,B_1]})\n1_{M_{2,2}(\\mathbb Z_p)}(Y_{[B_2,f]}),\n\\qquad \\operatorname{supp}\\varphi_p\\subset\\operatorname{GL}_2(\\mathbb Z_p).\n\\]\nLet \\(J(s)\\) have upper block type \\((2,1,1)\\), full upper radical and final unit factors, head principal congruence of depth \\(s\\), and sufficiently deep lower blocks.</p><p>The canonical ordinary branch adds only finite étale character-group flags and frames in the rank-three multiplicative subgroup. It does not ramify the center Fourier parameter. Every component meets a compatible ordinary cusp, and Fourier–Jacobi expansions detect integral divisibility without a precision loss depending on \\(s\\), also after finite coefficient extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cartier duality turns multiplicative flags and frames into finite étale character-group data; the full upper radical removes choices of étale-quotient lifts. Normalization and ordinary completion retain the corresponding branch.</p><p>At a rank-one boundary, the multiplicative torsion and its character flags are constructed before quotienting by the Raynaud period lattice, so they are independent of the center Fourier parameter. Boundary covers are finite étale covers of abelian extension-data charts; after pointing, addition lifts and makes them isogenies of abelian schemes.</p><p>Each connected finite étale component surjects onto its base component and meets its ordinary cusps. Reduction of expansions detects zero sections; repeated uniformizer division gives saturation. These arguments use étale geometry, not the degree of the cover.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.4", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, “The two \\(p\\)-operators.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\( U_j=[J(s_i)\\operatorname{diag}(1_j,p^{-1}1_{4-j})J(s_i)], \\qquad j=2,3, \\) the unnormalized action on \\(\\Phi_p\\) is \\(p^2\\).", "statement": "<p>For\n\\[\nU_j=[J(s_i)\\operatorname{diag}(1_j,p^{-1}1_{4-j})J(s_i)],\n\\qquad j=2,3,\n\\]\nthe unnormalized action on \\(\\Phi_p\\) is \\(p^2\\). In weight \\((m,m,1;-1)\\), the integral ordinary normalization is \\(p^{-2}U_j\\), and both comparison values are one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Upper shifts are indexed by \\(M_{j,4-j}(\\mathbb F_p)\\). Rank two of the head leaves exactly \\(p^{(j-2)(4-j)}\\) shifts making the expanding columns divisible by \\(p\\). The head determinant character is unchanged. The split Weil action gives \\(p^{4-j}\\); their product is \\(p^{(j-1)(4-j)}=p^2\\) for \\(j=2,3\\), independently of depth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.5", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, “Bounded cuts of unary moments.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix integral position lattices, additive characters, Igusa frames, and finite tests away from \\(p\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix integral position lattices, additive characters, Igusa frames, and finite tests away from \\(p\\). Moving tame tests may have bounded group-ring coefficients and \\(p\\)-unit Fourier normalizations.</p><p>For any bounded locally constant \\(p\\)-position cut \\(c\\), of unrestricted conductor, and finite polynomial combination \\(P\\) of normalized position moments,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal J(cP)|_p\\leq C\\|P\\|_{\\mathrm{pos}}.\n\\]\nThe constant depends only on fixed data, not moment degrees, cut conductor, moving levels, or coefficient extensions. The same bound holds at canonical cusps.</p><p>The uncut functional is a bounded measure on the compact position lattice. A sign-invariant cut multiplies that measure by \\(c\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Work coefficientwise for finite group algebras; ultrametric sums introduce no group-order loss. Fix a finite list of cuts and moments and put it on one sufficiently deep canonical Hilbert Igusa level.</p><p>Every component has a detecting cusp compatible with the fixed multiplicative position frame. Its comparison matrix has lower-left entry in \\(p^s\\mathbb Z_p\\), and\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}d^{-1}&amp;b\\\\0&amp;d\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\c/d&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAt sufficient depth the second factor fixes the tests. Thus effective changes lie in the upper triangular group times the stabilizer: position rescaling by units, unit phases, and test permutations. A Fourier interchange is not a compatible canonical-branch change.</p><p>The polynomial and cut transform in the same coordinate. Each cusp coefficient is\n\\[\n\\sum_{Q(x)=\\beta}\\varphi(x)c(x)P(x),\n\\]\na finite sum bounded by the fixed test norm times \\(\\|P\\|_{\\mathrm{pos}}\\). Away-from-\\(p\\) Fourier constants have only fixed denominators; moving coefficients remain integral.</p><p>To apply saturation to analytic jets, enlarge the finite multiplier level so products of base half-weight theta functions are algebraic integral-weight forms. Leibniz and Gauss–Manin express products of normalized jets through their jets; local square-root frames show no horizontal poles. Ordinary unit-root projection makes the square of a finite linear combination a tower element with \\(p\\) inverted. At ordinary CM objects the unit-root and CM splittings agree.</p><p>Its cusp expansions, after one fixed predicted clearing, are integral. A minimal positive vertical denominator would make every reduced expansion zero; saturation on the canonical Hilbert branch would divide it further, a contradiction. Taking square roots of valuations gives the stated bound, with no cut-dependent denominator remaining.</p><p>For arbitrary cuts, use the norm-one parity projectors, since \\(2\\) is invertible. The parity bases have position factors \\(x^{e_0}Q(x)^j\\), so the same argument applies. Polynomial density extends the uncut functional to a bounded measure. Approximate sign-invariant cuts by symmetrized polynomials; the same bound identifies insertion with multiplication of the measure.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.6", "orig": "Proposition 7.6, “Integral theta sections.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is one \\(c\\geq0\\) such that \\(p^c\\Theta_{i,m}\\) is an integral ordinary section on the canonical branch, coefficientwise in \\(\\mathcal O[G_i]\\), for all increasing depths, sufficiently large positive branch …", "statement": "<p>There is one \\(c\\geq0\\) such that \\(p^c\\Theta_{i,m}\\) is an integral ordinary section on the canonical branch, coefficientwise in \\(\\mathcal O[G_i]\\), for all increasing depths, sufficiently large positive branch weights, fixed variants, and bounded head cuts retaining determinant equivariance on a fixed compact.</p><p>Its Fourier–Jacobi coefficients are integral in the monomial-dual differential lattice.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose canonical cusp representatives integral at \\(p\\) and preserving the deep multiplicative flag. Fourier-dualizing the trailing \\(f\\)-column introduces no nonunit normalization: integral-translation invariance remains, because head changes occur only at the retained depth and trailing tests remain integral.</p><p>Partition the other tests into cosets and products of unary tests. Lemma 15.5 bounds their monomial moments uniformly. The column-degree filtration, integral logarithmic frames, and prime-to-\\(p\\) Jacobi torsion comparisons identify these with the mixed coefficients.</p><p>Boundary approximation retains all finite conditions. Source determinant equivariance cancels the character on a fixed compact, so source volume denominators are fixed. Lemma 15.3’s saturation yields integral sections with the same clearing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.7", "orig": "Lemma 7.7, “CM translations and bounded Fourier projection.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In connected–étale coordinates \\(e_p,f_p\\), right translation by \\(n(j/p^b)\\) gives \\( \\mathbb Z_pe_p+\\mathbb Z_p(f_p+(j/p^b)e_p), \\) corresponding to Serre–Tate parameter \\(\\zeta_{p^b}^{cj}\\), for fixed …", "statement": "<p>In connected–étale coordinates \\(e_p,f_p\\), right translation by \\(n(j/p^b)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb Z_pe_p+\\mathbb Z_p(f_p+(j/p^b)e_p),\n\\]\ncorresponding to Serre–Tate parameter \\(\\zeta_{p^b}^{cj}\\), for fixed \\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\), and preserving the connected differential integrally.</p><p>If \\(F(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb Z_p}z^y\\,d\\mu(y)\\), \\(\\chi\\) has conductor dividing \\(p^b\\), and \\(\\delta\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\), then\n\\[\np^{-b}\\sum_{j\\bmod p^b}\\sum_{x\\in(\\mathbb Z/p^b)^\\times}\n\\chi^{-1}(x)\\zeta_{p^b}^{-xj}F(\\zeta_{p^b}^{c\\delta j})\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb Z_p^\\times}\\chi^{-1}(c\\delta y)\\,d\\mu(y).\n\\]\nIts norm is at most \\(\\|\\mu\\|\\), independently of \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Pulling an ordinary extension back by \\(p^b\\) on its étale generator splits it; compatible roots shift an étale lift by \\(j/p^b\\) in the connected direction. The lattice meets the connected rational line in \\(\\mathbb Z_pe_p\\) and projects to \\(\\mathbb Z_pf_p\\), so connected differential transport is integral, not scaled by \\(p^b\\).</p><p>Substitute the measure transform. The sum over \\(j\\) is \\(p^b\\) exactly when \\(x\\equiv c\\delta y\\pmod{p^b}\\), and zero otherwise. There is one unit \\(x\\) exactly for unit \\(y\\). This cancels \\(p^{-b}\\) and proves the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.8", "orig": "Proposition 7.8, “The toric weight limits.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(P_{0,i}(m)\\), \\(P_{1,i}(m)\\) be the raw, unaveraged toric periods for \\(E,E^{D'}\\), using the Mellin-one test …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P_{0,i}(m)\\), \\(P_{1,i}(m)\\) be the raw, unaveraged toric periods for \\(E,E^{D'}\\), using the Mellin-one test\n\\[\nW_{\\chi_p^{\\mathrm{Mell}}}(a(x))\n=(\\chi_p^{\\mathrm{Mell}})^{-1}(x)1_{\\mathbb Z_p^\\times}(x),\n\\qquad \\chi_p^{\\mathrm{Mell}}=\\eta_p^{-1},\n\\]\nand the stated moving and fixed local tests.</p><p>At fixed stage their limits as positive \\(m\\to0\\) \\(p\\)-adically and \\(m\\to\\infty\\) ordinarily exist coefficientwise and are uniformly bounded as \\(i\\) varies. Up to fixed nonzero scalar and group-like units, the first limit is the stage image of \\(b_w^0\\).</p><p>Thus weights may be chosen with\n\\[\nP_{0,i}(m_i)(z_0)\\to0,\\qquad\n\\sup_i|P_{1,i}(m_i)(z_0)|_p&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nwith arbitrary additional stagewise approximation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite Fourier inversion expresses the Mellin-one test as\n\\[\np^{-b_p}\\sum_{j\\bmod p^{b_p}}\n\\left(\\sum_{x\\in(\\mathbb Z/p^{b_p})^\\times}\n(\\chi_p^{\\mathrm{Mell}})^{-1}(x)\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}}(-xj/p^{b_p})\\right)\nn(j/p^{b_p})W_0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(W_0(a(x))=1_{\\mathbb Z_p^\\times}(x)\\).</p><p>At fixed stage and depth, CM/Katz comparison identifies the weight limit with \\(F_0=d_{\\mathrm{mod}}^{-1}f^{[p]}\\) in fixed Igusa frames. Lemma 15.7 preserves the frames and evaluates the Fourier projector by a bounded unit-restriction moment. Reciprocity gives the label\n\\[\n[-\\eta_\\Gamma(y)-\\eta_\\Gamma(\\delta_{\\mathfrak a})]\n[t_{\\mathfrak a}]\\psi_s(\\mathfrak a),\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the undepleted series of Proposition 12, up to fixed translation conventions.</p><p>For the companion, the same calculation and bounded inverse-derivative measures give a uniform bound. Raw conductor sums and prime-to-\\(p\\) translates preserve it.</p><p>No uniform inverse Fourier norm is asserted: at each fixed stage its finite loss is paid by taking weight sufficiently close to zero before increasing the stage. The limiting polynomials themselves have the proved uniform bound. Interior substitution respects bounded limits, giving the asserted choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition 8.1, “Theta implication.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R=\\mathbb Z_p[[t,\\mathbf u]]\\) satisfy the imaginary auxiliary conditions, and let \\(\\mathfrak p\\) be horizontal.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(V=V_pE\\), \\(V'=V_pE^{D'}\\), with universal scalar \\(\\chi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(a\\in\\{w,\\bar w\\}\\), let \\(H^1_{\\mathrm{prim},a}\\) be degree-one cohomology of the marked complex strict at \\(a\\), full at the other \\(p\\)-place, unramified at fixed non-\\(p\\) places, and with permitted full moving conditions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(b_w^0\\in\\mathfrak p\\), then over \\(\\operatorname{Frac}(R/\\mathfrak p)\\), at least one of\n\\[\nH^1_{\\mathrm{prim},a}(K,V\\chi),\\qquad\nH^1_{\\mathrm{prim},a}(K,V'\\chi)\n\\]\nis nonzero. The fixed side \\(a\\) is determined by the global reciprocity convention. Replacing \\(w\\) by \\(\\bar w\\) exchanges sides. Both alternatives use the scalar character of the determinant problem.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume both divisor-field groups vanish. Lemma 15.2 chooses \\(z_0\\) retaining this vanishing. Propositions 15 and Lemmas 16.1–16.4 produce cusp congruences with bounded nonzero content, increasing precision, and the necessary local Galois controls.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nM=D_{fy}\\otimes_{\\mathscr T,\\lambda}k.\n\\]\nFitting base change and Lemma 16.7 give \\(M\\neq0\\). A nonzero functional \\(M\\to k\\) gives an extension of \\(y\\) by \\(f\\), keeping \\(x\\) separate. Lemma 16.6 ensures the only additional path relation:\n\\[\nD_{fx}D_{xy}=D_{fx}D_{xf}D_{fy},\n\\]\nwhose crossing cycle is killed by \\(\\lambda\\). Thus this is an algebra representation and a nonzero admissible class in\n\\[\nH^1(L,V\\chi^{\\pm1}).\n\\]\nA splitting would be preserved by the full algebra and idempotents and kill the functional.</p><p>The common-kernel minor argument makes it strict at the conjugate half of the \\(p\\)-places, and full at the other half. At binary-controlled fixed places, the inertia-fixed projection lifts the \\(y\\)-quotient, giving unramifiedness.</p><p>At an unramified-twist Steinberg place, write inertia as\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}F(\\iota)&amp;c(\\iota)\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nF(\\iota)=1+t_p(\\iota)N,\\quad N^2=0,\\quad\\operatorname{rank}N=1.\n\\]\nParahoric two-by-two minors imply \\(c(\\iota)\\in\\operatorname{im}N\\) whenever \\(t_p(\\iota)\\neq0\\), and the cocycle law gives the same for all inertia. Hence \\(c\\) is an additive continuous homomorphism into that fixed line, factors through tame pro-\\(p\\) inertia, and equals \\(t_p(\\iota)v\\). Choose \\(b\\) with \\(Nb=v\\); then \\(c(\\iota)=(F(\\iota)-1)b\\), an inertia coboundary. Other fixed places are hyperspecial; moving places keep full conditions.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_{G_L}^{G_K}(V\\chi^{\\pm1}|_{G_L})\n\\simeq V\\chi^{\\pm1}\\oplus V'\\chi^{\\pm1}.\n\\]\nShapiro and the marked diagrams preserve strict/full \\(p\\)-conditions and unramified fixed conditions. Finite-index inertia restriction detects unramifiedness in characteristic zero. At least one primitive component is nonzero. If the inverse scalar occurs, conjugation converts it to \\(\\chi\\) on the opposite fixed side. This contradicts the choice of \\(z_0\\) and proves the theta implication.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Positive content before restoring local controls; argument of original Sections 8.1–8.2.", "statement": "<p>Positive content before restoring local controls; argument of original Sections 8.1–8.2.</p><p>Assume, for contradiction, that both primitive groups in Proposition 16 vanish generically, and choose \\(z_0\\) by Lemma 15.2. Use the nonzero stabilizer period and unary periods from Lemma 15.1.</p><p>There are positive integer degrees \\(d_{1i},d_{2i}\\), both tending ordinarily to infinity, with\n\\[\nm_i=d_{1i}+d_{2i}+1\\to0\n\\]\non the chosen \\(p\\)-adic branch, such that the one-index theta unfolding has evaluated positive Fourier–Jacobi content of uniformly bounded valuation at \\(z_0\\). These degrees can satisfy Proposition 15’s stagewise weight approximations.</p>", "proof": "<p>At primitive index \\(e_1\\), use the monomial\n\\[\n[e,B_1]^{d_1}[e,B_2]^{d_2}.\n\\]\nAt \\(p\\), put the unary unit test in the row-two \\(B_1\\)-position and retain the \\(B_2\\) integer test; the head determinant character cancels the source weight without shrinking the compact.</p><p>At each moving prime introduce \\(v'_j\\) and split the global character into parameters \\(v'_j\\) and \\((1+u_j)/(1+v'_j)-1\\). Require both heads invertible and insert their determinant characters. Partition trailing matrices modulo the moving uniformizer and subtract their residue representatives. This expresses the tests as bounded integral sums of pivot translates, with only \\(p\\)-unit scales and no averaging.</p><p>The one-index unfolding separates a row-one Heisenberg theta function. Averaging the two unary slots against their inverse characters gives\n\\[\nI_{\\mathrm{stab}}T_1(d_1)T_2(d_2).\n\\]\nUnit equivariance makes the averaging denominator fixed. Fixed torus representatives and prime-to-\\(p\\) Jacobi torsions detect content in the monomial-dual ordinary lattice.</p><p>The nonaugmentation issue is handled by the product-character measure identity. With moving parameters zero and moving cuts temporarily omitted, write \\(d=d_{10}+hn\\), where \\(h\\) kills finite and torsion factors of \\(\\widehat{\\mathcal C}\\), and set \\(\\mathcal C_\\Gamma=\\widehat{\\mathcal C}^{\\,h}\\). There are a bounded measure \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\) and nowhere-zero analytic unit \\(u(d)\\) such that the first unary period is\n\\[\nu(d)\\,\\mu(\\mathcal C_\\Gamma^n\\nu_t).\n\\]\nTo obtain this identity, express the torus integral using fixed representatives with \\(p\\)-component one, move their finite Schwartz tests to one fixed CM object and frame, and push the bounded position measures forward under\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto r^{-1}\\vartheta(x)\\in\\Gamma.\n\\]\nThe degree power and true-character factor are the same character evaluated on this image. Thus no representative-dependent degree power is left outside the measure.</p><p>For\n\\[\nF(Z)=\\int_\\Gamma(1+Z)^{\\ell(g)}\\,d\\mu(g),\n\\]\nunary nonvanishing gives \\(F\\neq0\\). At fixed \\(t=t_0\\), its argument is\n\\[\nZ(n)=(1+t_0)\\mathcal C_\\Gamma(\\gamma)^n-1.\n\\]\nThe nonzero logarithmic direction makes \\(Z(n)\\) nonconstant on every degree disk; analytic uniqueness shows that the first period is not identically zero in degree. The second is nonzero from its original nonvanishing value.</p><p>Restoring moving unit cuts multiplies periods by factors\n\\[\n1-\\alpha_v\\mathcal C(\\gamma_v)^{\\pm d},\n\\]\neach nonzero analytic because the weight values are nontorsion. Hence\n\\[\nT_1(a;\\mathbf v'),\\qquad\nT_2(-1-a;(1+\\mathbf u_0)/(1+\\mathbf v')-1)\n\\]\nare nonzero analytic functions: test the first at \\(\\mathbf v'=0\\), the second at \\(\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf u_0\\). Their product is nonzero. Choose \\(a\\in\\mathbb Z_p\\) and \\(\\mathbf v'\\) in a fixed finite extension where it is nonzero.</p><p>Varying coefficient fields are handled by the completed field of bounded \\(\\mathbb C_p\\)-arrays modulo zero ultralimit norm. A nonzero value records a uniform valuation bound on an ultrafilter-large stage set. Lemma 15.5 gives uniform tails and permits the analytic limits and the chosen fixed-field evaluations.</p><p>Choose \\(d_{1i}\\to a\\), \\(d_{2i}\\to-1-a\\) with arbitrarily accurate stage congruences and arbitrarily large ordinary sizes. The compatible branches make \\(m_i\\) meet every requirement of Proposition 15, while the displayed product has bounded valuation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, “Content with the local controls.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are controlled theta sections at \\(z_0\\), of the preceding weights and increasing depths, whose constant terms tend uniformly to zero and whose ordinary positive Fourier–Jacobi content has valuation bounded above …", "statement": "<p>There are controlled theta sections at \\(z_0\\), of the preceding weights and increasing depths, whose constant terms tend uniformly to zero and whose ordinary positive Fourier–Jacobi content has valuation bounded above independently of stage.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 16.1 gives temporary content. Restore fixed split controls by [23, Lemma 6.4]. Auxiliary-good spanning denominators are nonzero at \\(z_0\\). At additive places, supercuspidal heads have no proper Jacquet modules; ramified principal or special heads cannot match the unramified lower-rank orbit characters. The open-orbit induction is unlinked and generated by a nontrivially paired pivot. A finite-level nonzero spanning minor and Cramer’s rule give finite denominators at \\(z_0\\).</p><p>Cross-multiply before group-algebra evaluation. Clearing factors have nonzero limits with bounded inverse valuations. Prime-to-\\(p\\) translations preserve ordinary integrality, including long bounded integral moving sums without averaging. If all controlled contents tended to zero, their temporary expressions would too, contradicting Lemma 16.1. Choose one with bounded content at each stage.</p><p>Retain the two positive binary operators and full final spherical block at each binary control. Proposition 15 applies simultaneously to these bounded variants, giving constant-term vanishing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, “Uniform cusp congruence.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After one fixed precision loss, the controlled sections give finite integral operator orders \\(\\mathbb T_i^0\\) on classical cusp spaces and maps …", "statement": "<p>After one fixed precision loss, the controlled sections give finite integral operator orders \\(\\mathbb T_i^0\\) on classical cusp spaces and maps\n\\[\n\\lambda_i^0:\\mathbb T_i^0\\to\\mathcal O_k/(p^{M_i}),\n\\qquad M_i\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThey have the theta comparison values on split good and controlled operators, and value one on both normalized \\(p\\)-operators. No bound on the depth \\(s_i\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>At each depth, the canonical branch and its boundary covers are finite étale on multiplicative character data, with unchanged center Fourier parameter. Positive-index Jacobi line bundles are ample on the abelian charts. Their differential bundles have integral filtrations with constant graded pieces, so positive cohomology vanishes and positive coefficients lift modulo \\(p^M\\), also on the finite étale covers. Neatness removes stabilizer denominators; formal functions identifies the lifts with the cusp sheaf.</p><p>Multiply by sufficiently divisible Hasse powers to extend across the nonordinary complement and use Serre vanishing for characteristic-zero lifting. Choose shifts \\(H_i\\) arbitrarily large ordinarily and tending to zero on weight branches. The resulting weights are\n\\[\n(m_i+H_i,m_i+H_i,1+H_i;-1-H_i).\n\\]</p><p>The graph trace for \\(U_j\\), adjoining \\(u_a^p=q_a\\), is divisible by \\(p^j\\). Flags depend on the graph, not root choices. With the differential bounds, the operators\n\\[\n\\widetilde U_2=p^{-(2+\\lambda'_3+\\lambda'_4)}U_2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde U_3=p^{-(3+\\lambda'_4)}U_3\n\\]\npreserve the integral lattice. Hasse shifts alter differential and determinant factors identically; sufficiently divisible shifts leave comparison values one.</p><p>Intersect the classical cusp space with ordinary integral sections to get a full saturated bounded lattice. Its commuting operators generate a finite torsion-free \\(\\mathcal O_k\\)-order. An integral operator relation applied to the lifted section gives its comparison scalar times that section zero modulo the retained precision. A positive coefficient of bounded valuation cancels the content with one fixed loss. This factors scalar evaluation through the entire order and selects the residual local summand where both normalized operators are units.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, “Binary separation at an inertia test.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On an actual cusp eigensystem, let \\(\\rho_\\pi\\) be its four-dimensional Galois representation.", "statement": "<p>On an actual cusp eigensystem, let \\(\\rho_\\pi\\) be its four-dimensional Galois representation. Suppose a chosen inertia element \\(\\iota_v\\) has characteristic polynomial\n\\[\nP_{\\iota_v}(Z)=(Z-1)^2C(Z),\\qquad \\Delta_v=C(1)\\neq0,\n\\]\nwith the two unit eigenvalues allocated to the final unramified binary block. Then its generalized \\(1\\)-primary projection \\(P_v^{\\mathrm{bin}}\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(\\rho_\\pi(\\iota)-1)P_v^{\\mathrm{bin}}=0\n\\qquad(\\iota\\in I_v).\n\\]</p><p>Together with Frobenius-separated controls, there is an exponent \\(C_0\\), independent of stage and depth, such that\n\\[\n\\Delta^{C_0}\\ker(\\mathbb T_i^0\\to\\mathbb T_i)=0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbb T_i\\) is the image order in characteristic-zero eigensystems and \\(\\Delta\\) is the product of separation factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the Galois package [23, Proposition 8.5]. Its representations satisfy\n\\[\n\\rho_\\pi^c\\simeq\\rho_\\pi^\\vee\\epsilon^{-3},\n\\]\nwith distinguished Hodge weights\n\\[\nh_1=1-m_i-H_i,\\quad h_2=2-m_i-H_i,\\quad\nh_3=2-H_i,\\quad h_4=1+H_i.\n\\]\nThe two unit operator values force Newton contacts at dimensions two and three. Strict Hodge gaps give strict Newton gaps; weak admissibility makes those canonical subspaces weakly admissible and preserves descent. Thus there is a stable flag with last quotient\n\\[\nX_w=\\epsilon^{-h_4}\\operatorname{unr}(\\widetilde U_3^{-1}),\n\\qquad\nY_{\\bar w}=X_w^{-c}\\epsilon^{-3}\n\\]\nas first conjugate subline, tending uniformly to \\(x=\\epsilon^{-1}\\), \\(y=\\epsilon^{-2}\\).</p><p>At an inertia-separated binary place, the whole inertia-trivial Weil summand is the final binary allocation. No norm twist changes inertia, so no segment crosses to the head. The predicted binary monodromy is zero. Actual monodromy also vanishes: the Galois construction is a direct sum of shifted generic unitary factors; selected length-one parameters cannot be linked by a norm ratio, since their real-exponent interval has width less than one. Hence the binary subspace is inertia-fixed.</p><p>Explicitly,\n\\[\nP_v^{\\mathrm{bin}}=\nC(\\rho_\\pi(\\iota_v))\n\\left(\\Delta_v^{-1}-C'(1)\\Delta_v^{-2}(\\rho_\\pi(\\iota_v)-1)\\right).\n\\]\nOnly powers of \\(\\Delta_v\\) occur in denominators.</p><p>For the order, the geometric-lemma filtration has at most \\(4!\\) allocation terms at each controlled place. Separation forces the final binary allocation, whose spherical invariants are one-dimensional and whose two operators are scalar. At \\(p\\), the contacts force the \\((2,1,1)\\) allocation independently of depth. Thus separated generalized spaces have one grade and no operator nilpotents. On components where \\(\\Delta\\) has zero eigenvalue, it lowers the bounded filtration; a fixed power kills them. Torsion-freeness transfers the characteristic-zero identity to the integral order.</p><p>Since \\(\\lambda_i^0(\\Delta)\\) has bounded valuation, cancel this fixed loss to obtain maps\n\\[\n\\lambda_i:\\mathbb T_i\\to\\mathcal O_k/(p^{M_i}),\\qquad M_i\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nAt line parahorics, retain also unipotent inertia with \\(\\operatorname{rank}(\\rho_\\pi(\\iota)-1)\\leq1\\), from the monodromy bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Limiting corner algebra; construction of original Section 8.5.", "statement": "<p>Limiting corner algebra; construction of original Section 8.5.</p><p>For the orders and representations above, there is a henselian local \\(k\\)-algebra \\(\\mathscr T\\), residue map \\(\\lambda:\\mathscr T\\to k\\), and finite matrix algebra \\(\\mathscr B\\), faithful in matrices over a reduced absolutely flat ambient ring, with diagonal blocks\n\\[\nf=V(-2)\\chi^{\\pm1}|_{G_L},\\qquad x=\\epsilon^{-1},\\qquad y=\\epsilon^{-2}.\n\\]\nThe blocks are absolutely simple and pairwise distinct.</p><p>There are finite single-entry corner modules \\(D_{uv}\\), \\(u,v\\in\\{f,x,y\\}\\), with diagonal corners \\(\\mathscr T\\) and\n\\[\n\\lambda(D_{uv}D_{vu})=0\\qquad(u\\neq v).\n\\]\nAlgebra maps from \\(\\mathscr B\\) to matrices over \\(k\\) produce admissible cohomology classes in the marked finite-model field tests, preserving specified local conditions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(B_i=\\mathbb T_i[\\rho_i]\\). Residual semisimplifications are fixed, since varying characters and branch differences reduce trivially. After a fixed extension the residual images are pro-\\(p\\); bounded support cardinality bounds their generators. Cayley–Hamilton and the four-by-four polynomial-identity bound give finitely many algebra monomials uniformly generating \\(B_i\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal A=\\prod_{\\mathcal U}\\mathbb T_i\\), let \\(\\mathfrak q\\) be the kernel of its compact map to \\(\\mathcal O_k\\), and set\n\\[\n\\mathscr T=(\\mathcal A_{\\mathfrak q})^h,\\qquad\n\\mathscr B=(\\prod_{\\mathcal U}B_i)\\otimes_{\\mathcal A}\\mathscr T.\n\\]\nThe ambient product of algebraically closed coefficient fields is reduced absolutely flat; flat localization and étale base change preserve faithful embeddings. Its field evaluations are realized by residue ultraproducts of actual systems.</p><p>The theta polynomial comparisons at split good Frobenius, uniform convergence of weight characters, and stagewise Frobenius approximation give the diagonal blocks on all sequences of group elements. Lift four rank-one idempotents by henselian factorization and inverse matrix units for the two elliptic positions. The trace pairing gives the diagonal representation and kills crossing cycles under \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>The corner-minor identity follows by decomposing determinant monomials into a surplus-column-to-surplus-row path and diagonal cycles. It transports stacked zero minors to the corner algebra.</p><p>A matrix algebra map has one common denominator on the bounded monomial generators. Stage coefficient expressions and \\(\\lambda_i\\) give continuous matrix functions to increasing uniform precision. Relations on all sequences are uniformly valid, or a violating sequence would contradict them.</p><p>Use Proposition 3’s contractions \\(I_i,Q_i,H_i\\). Apply \\(Q_i\\) to an admissible cocycle after clearing its fixed denominator; its defect tends uniformly to zero. Bounded model rank and compact coefficient limits give an actual model cocycle. The contraction homotopy identifies it with the original admissible class, and detects coboundaries in both directions. The marked global-local cone retains every local splitting condition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.6", "orig": "Lemma 8.5, “The cyclotomic corner.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Lemma 16.5’s corner algebra, \\( D_{xy}=D_{xf}D_{fy}. \\)", "statement": "<p>In Lemma 16.5’s corner algebra,\n\\[\nD_{xy}=D_{xf}D_{fy}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A functional on\n\\[\nD_{xy}/(D_{xf}D_{fy}+\\mathfrak mD_{xy})\n\\]\ngives an extension of \\(y\\) by \\(x\\), with separate \\(f\\)-block. If its Galois class splits, the algebra and corner idempotents preserve the splitting, forcing the functional to vanish. Thus this quotient injects into admissible \\(H^1(L,k(1))\\).</p><p>At \\(\\bar w\\), the common local \\(Y_{\\bar w}\\)-kernel gives zero stacked four-row determinants. The retained local invariant vanishings give an invertible competing-column minor on \\(f_1,f_2,x\\), hence a local \\(y\\)-eigenline. The transposed argument at \\(w\\), using the common quotient \\(X_w\\), also splits the extension. It is strict at every \\(p\\)-place.</p><p>Binary projections make it unramified at binary places. At a line parahoric, two-by-two minors give an elliptic inertia difference times its extension entry equal to zero; the cocycle law then kills the entry on all inertia. Other places are hyperspecial. Admissible comparison descends to the fixed field \\(L\\).</p><p>Kummer theory identifies the resulting group with \\(p\\)-units whose valuations and logarithms vanish at all \\(p\\)-places. Valuations leave global units. The CM biquadratic field \\(L\\) has unit rank one, and its nontrivial fundamental unit has nonzero \\(p\\)-adic logarithm at a split \\(p\\)-place. Thus the class is zero. Nakayama gives the corner identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.7", "orig": "Lemma 8.6, “Exclusion of the last stable line at arbitrary depth.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The image of \\(D_{fy}\\) is nonzero in every characteristic-zero field evaluation of the ambient ring.", "statement": "<p>The image of \\(D_{fy}\\) is nonzero in every characteristic-zero field evaluation of the ambient ring. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Fitt}_{0,\\mathscr T}(D_{fy})=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(D_{fy}\\) vanished in such a field, Lemma 16.6 would also kill \\(D_{xy}\\), making the last idempotent line globally stable. The invertible competing minor forces its conjugate local character to be \\(Y_{\\bar w}\\).</p><p>These finite generator, idempotent, matrix-unit, and minor relations hold on a filter-large set of actual systems. Each would have a global one-dimensional constituent \\(\\xi\\) restricting to \\(Y_{\\bar w}\\). It is de Rham and algebraic Hecke; its conjugate weight sums are parallel. Its isolated lowest conjugate weight excludes Arthur length greater than one. Strict generic unitary bounds force its integer conjugate weight sum to be three.</p><p>Thus \\(\\xi\\) has weight \\(h_4\\) at \\(w\\). It cannot lie in the rank-three local subrepresentation with weights \\(h_1,h_2,h_3\\); its projection to the last quotient is an isomorphism, so its entire local character is \\(X_w\\), without a conductor bound. Therefore\n\\[\n\\xi(g)\\xi(g^c)=\\epsilon(g)^{-3}.\n\\]\nChoose fixed inertia \\(g\\) with nontorsion \\(\\epsilon(g)\\). The corresponding last-corner expression is zero in the field evaluation, but its residue is\n\\[\n(\\epsilon(g)^{-4}-\\epsilon(g)^{-3})e_y\\neq0,\n\\]\nso it is a unit, a contradiction.</p><p>Any annihilator of \\(D_{fy}\\) vanishes in every ambient field and is zero by reducedness and faithfulness. The zeroth Fitting ideal is contained in the annihilator, giving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Lemma 9.2, “Equality of the two quotient ideals.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every imaginary auxiliary tuple satisfying the preceding requirements, \\( (U_w)=(U_{\\bar w}) \\) in \\(R=\\mathbb Z_p[[t,\\mathbf u]]\\).", "statement": "<p>For every imaginary auxiliary tuple satisfying the preceding requirements,\n\\[\n(U_w)=(U_{\\bar w})\n\\]\nin \\(R=\\mathbb Z_p[[t,\\mathbf u]]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Factoriality and Lemma 17.1 make each quotient nonzero modulo \\((p,t)\\). Proposition 14 makes their high true-character specializations associates after inverting \\(p\\). Apply Proposition 5’s integral division twice, in opposite directions, using faithful flatness to descend the zero remainders. Thus \\(U_w\\mid U_{\\bar w}\\) and \\(U_{\\bar w}\\mid U_w\\), including all multiplicities.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At every horizontal height-one prime, primitive one-sided complexes for \\(V_0=V_pE\\) and \\(V_1=V_pE^{D'}\\) are perfect of amplitude \\([1,2]\\) and Euler characteristic zero.", "statement": "<p>At every horizontal height-one prime, primitive one-sided complexes for \\(V_0=V_pE\\) and \\(V_1=V_pE^{D'}\\) are perfect of amplitude \\([1,2]\\) and Euler characteristic zero.</p><p>If \\(M_{a,w}\\) denotes the primitive determinant and \\(D_a\\) the fixed singular Euler product, then\n\\[\nL_{0,w}=D_0M_{0,w},\\qquad L_{1,w}=D_1M_{1,w}\n\\]\nup to DVR units.</p><p>Every irreducible divisor of \\(U_w\\) or \\(U_{\\bar w}\\) is horizontal and has nonzero reduction modulo \\((p,t)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(p\\), true inertia and artificial Frobenius equations have no common height-one component, so local \\(H^0,H^2\\) vanish. At a moving place, inertia forces its variable zero, and the chosen central Frobenius has no eigenvalue one on that divisor. Global invariants vanish by absolute simplicity.</p><p>Fixed unramified conditions include \\(H^0\\) and are exact complements. Duality and Euler characteristic give amplitude and equal ranks. The full-to-unramified quotient is residue cohomology on inertia coinvariants with twist \\((-1)\\), giving the determinant identities. Thus \\(v(M_{a,w})\\geq0\\), and a determinant vanishes precisely at a one-sided jump.</p><p>Proposition 12 gives\n\\[\nU_w=\\text{unit}\\cdot(b_w^0)^2/M_{0,w}.\n\\]\nA positive quotient valuation forces \\(b_w^0\\) to vanish. Proposition 16 gives a one-sided jump for one representation, so the prime divides one of the four \\(L_{a,w}\\). Proposition 6 makes each nonzero modulo \\((p,t)\\), hence every factor also has nonzero reduction because \\(R/(p,t)\\) is a domain.</p><p>Finally Proposition 13 and \\(B_w=L_wU_w\\) give \\(p\\nmid U_w\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Proposition 9.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathfrak q\\) range over the finitely many old horizontal factors of \\(U_wU_{\\bar w}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Enlarge \\(R\\) to \\(S=R[[y_1,\\ldots,y_n]]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At\n  \\[\n  A_{\\mathfrak q}=S_{(\\mathfrak q,\\mathbf y)},\n  \\]\n  use primitive complexes with fixed non-\\(p\\) unramified conditions, moving full conditions, and designated strict \\(p\\)-places.</p><p class=\"li\">A jump means nonzero degree-one cohomology on a derived field fiber.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The new directions can be chosen so that, in every \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A_{\\mathfrak q}\\), both following loci have codimension at least two:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Both one-sided primitive complexes for one representation jump.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. One designated one-sided complex for each of \\(V_0,V_1\\) jumps.</p><p>Setting \\(\\mathbf y=0\\) recovers the old complexes. The analytic and determinant specialization identities remain integral up to units.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 7 to all old fields and both representations. New primes are acyclic at every test, have nonzero old exponents, and their logarithms vanish at all old support. Inflation and the marked comparisons recover the old complexes and preserve all cup nullhomotopies.</p><p>Conjugate self-duality makes minimal one-sided differentials alternating, and Proposition 6’s generic acyclicity makes every one-sided jump even-dimensional. If both-strict dimension is \\(s\\), duality gives both-full dimension \\(s+2\\). The two one-sided spaces intersect in the both-strict space. If \\(s=0\\), two nonzero even spaces cannot both fit inside a two-dimensional both-full space with zero intersection. Hence a simultaneous one-representation jump lies in the both-strict jump locus.</p><p>At an old field choose bases \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_s\\) of both-strict and \\(d_1,\\ldots,d_{s+2}\\) of conjugate both-full, with the first \\(s\\) conjugate to the \\(x_i\\). Scalar localization permits cup primitives. Proposition 6’s lift equation remains valid and additive across new directions; cross-prime residue cups vanish.</p><p>Joint-kernel evaluations span opposite simple tuples, distinguished by an infinite-order old inertia scalar. The commutator primitive calculation spans every skew entry in the first \\(s\\) columns and every entry in the last two columns. Thus the tangent slice is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Alt}_s\\oplus\\operatorname{Mat}_{s,2}.\n\\]\nAfter cancelling unit blocks over \\(A_{\\mathfrak q}\\), the both-strict model is \\(A^s\\to A^{s+2}\\) with zero residue. A first-order liftable vector passes all cup tests, so Lemma 18.2 applies over every field extension and gives codimension at least two. If \\(s=0\\), the degree-one minimal term is absent.</p><p>For different representations, the indicated one-sided dimensions \\(s_0,s_1\\) are even. If either is zero there is no joint jump. Otherwise the four opposite-twist planes are pairwise nonisomorphic: old inertia distinguishes opposite twists; \\(V_0|_{G_L}\\) is absolutely simple, so an isomorphism with its nontrivial \\(L/K\\)-quadratic twist would be scalar on \\(G_L\\) and equal its negative on the other coset. Joint spanning gives independent slices\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Alt}_{s_0}\\oplus\\operatorname{Alt}_{s_1}.\n\\]\nThe simultaneous part of Lemma 18.2 proves the second assertion.</p><p>Take the union of all finite direction lists. Every prime is acyclic at every old test, so each slice survives further enlargement. Nonzero old exponents preserve residual concentration. The retained norm and localization markings give Proposition 12’s integral specialization laws.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.3, “Augmented alternating matrices.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a characteristic-zero field \\(k\\) and \\(s\\geq1\\), the rank-defect locus of \\( [A\\mid B],\\qquad (A,B)\\in\\operatorname{Alt}_s(k)\\oplus\\operatorname{Mat}_{s,2}(k), \\) has codimension at least two.", "statement": "<p>For a characteristic-zero field \\(k\\) and \\(s\\geq1\\), the rank-defect locus of\n\\[\n[A\\mid B],\\qquad\n(A,B)\\in\\operatorname{Alt}_s(k)\\oplus\\operatorname{Mat}_{s,2}(k),\n\\]\nhas codimension at least two.</p><p>For positive even \\(s_0,s_1\\), the locus\n\\[\n\\det A_0=\\det A_1=0\n\\]\nin \\(\\operatorname{Alt}_{s_0}(k)\\oplus\\operatorname{Alt}_{s_1}(k)\\) also has codimension at least two.</p>", "proof": "<p>Introduce \\([v]\\in\\mathbb P^{s-1}\\). For fixed \\([v]\\), the equations \\(v^tA=0\\), \\(v^tB=0\\) impose \\((s-1)+2\\) independent linear conditions. The incidence variety has dimension two less than the full matrix space, and proper projection has image the rank-defect locus.</p><p>For positive even size, the alternating determinant is nonzero and nonconstant, so its zero locus is a hypersurface. The two independent coordinate sets give codimension two.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.4, “First-order tests and formal codimension.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\((A,\\mathfrak m,k)\\) be regular local with \\(\\operatorname{char}k=0\\), and \\(D:A^s\\to A^r\\), \\(r\\geq s\\), have zero residue.", "statement": "<p>Let \\((A,\\mathfrak m,k)\\) be regular local with \\(\\operatorname{char}k=0\\), and \\(D:A^s\\to A^r\\), \\(r\\geq s\\), have zero residue. Let \\(D_1\\) be its tangent linear part.</p><p>If a linear injection\n\\[\n\\iota:\\operatorname{Alt}_s(k)\\oplus\\operatorname{Mat}_{s,2}(k)\\to T_A\n\\]\nsatisfies, over every extension field,\n\\[\n\\ker D_1(\\iota(A_0,B))\\subseteq\\ker[A_0\\mid B]^t,\n\\]\nthen the maximal-minor ideal \\(I_s(D)\\) has height at least two.</p><p>Likewise, for zero-residue maps \\(D_a:A^{s_a}\\to A^{r_a}\\), with positive even \\(s_a\\), independent tangent slices satisfying\n\\[\n\\ker(D_a)_1(\\iota(A_0,A_1))\\subseteq\\ker A_a\n\\]\nimply\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ht}(I_{s_0}(D_0)+I_{s_1}(D_1))\\geq2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The tangent rank-defect locus is a homogeneous closed cone. A codimension-one component would have a nonconstant homogeneous equation. Its restriction to the specified linear slice is either zero, forcing the whole slice into the defect locus, or a positive-degree polynomial, forcing a slice hypersurface into it. Both contradict Lemma 18.1.</p><p>Each nonzero maximal minor of the linear part is the corresponding degree-\\(s\\) initial form, so\n\\[\nI_s(D_1)\\subseteq\\operatorname{in}_{\\mathfrak m}I_s(D).\n\\]\nThe associated graded ring is polynomial, and initial ideals preserve quotient dimension. This proves the height bound. Apply the same cone and initial-ideal argument to the sum of tangent minor ideals for the simultaneous assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Theorem 9.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Both integral quotients are units:", "statement": "<p>Both integral quotients are units:\n\\[\nU_w,U_{\\bar w}\\in R^\\times.\n\\]\nRemoving every artificial variable gives an integral unit comparison between the analytic series and strict determinant on the genuine anticyclotomic line.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose Proposition 18’s enlargement and denote enlarged quotients by \\(\\widetilde U_w\\). Proposition 14 gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde U_w(t,\\mathbf u,0)\n=\n\\varepsilon_w(t,\\mathbf u)U_w(t,\\mathbf u),\n\\qquad \\varepsilon_w\\in R^\\times,\n\\]\nbecause numerator and denominator restore the same nonzero squared conductor factors and the determinant comparisons are integral.</p><p>Suppose an irreducible \\(F\\) divides an enlarged quotient. By Proposition 17’s divisor analysis it is horizontal. Its specialization \\(F_0\\) is nonzero, since it divides a nonzero specialized quotient, and is a nonunit, since a unit specialization would give a unit constant term for \\(F\\). Choose an irreducible factor \\(f\\) of \\(F_0\\). It is an old quotient divisor and is not \\(p\\). Since \\(F\\in(f,\\mathbf y)\\), the height-one prime \\((F)\\) survives in \\(S_{(f,\\mathbf y)}\\).</p><p>Proposition 17 for the enlarged tuple makes \\(F\\) divide both quotients. Their undepleted analytic series both vanish there. The conjugate theta implications of Proposition 16 then give either both-sided jumps for one representation or one one-sided jump for each representation. Proposition 18 excludes each possibility at height one.</p><p>Thus enlarged quotients are units, and their integral specializations make the old quotients units. If there were no old factors, no enlargement is needed.</p><p>Finally remove all artificial variables using the same identity. Restored factors may be nonunits at the center but are nonzero and have equal multiplicities in numerator and denominator. Cancellation leaves a unit in \\(\\mathbb Z_p[[t]]\\), with no precision loss.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Proposition 10.1 and Corollary 10.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p&gt;2\\), \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) with analytic rank zero or one, and the imaginary twist \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt D)\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_K\\) be the single Hilbert-class trace used in Proposition 12.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  n_P=v_p([E(K)/E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}:\\mathbb Z\\bar P_K]),\n  \\quad \\tau_g=v_p(\\#E(K)_{\\mathrm{tors}}),\n  \\quad s_K=v_p(\\#\\Sha(E/K)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\tau_q=v_p(\\#E(\\mathbb Q_q)[p^\\infty])\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\log_{\\omega_E}E(\\mathbb Q_p)=p^l\\mathbb Z_p.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_w^{s=1}(0)\\) be the actual central strict/full complex after removing artificial variables, and \\(L_w^{s=1}(0)\\) its differential determinant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n-v_p(L_w^{s=1}(0))\n=\n2n_P+2\\tau_g-s_K\n-2\\bigl(v_p(\\log_{\\omega_E}P_K)-l+\\tau_p\\bigr)\n-2\\sum_{q\\in S^\\circ}\\tau_q.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n2(n_P+\\tau_g)\n=\ns_K+2v_p(c_E)+2\\sum_{q\\ \\mathrm{finite}}v_p(c_q(E)),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nX_p(E)+X_p(E^D)=0.\n\\]\nThese assertions include CM curves.</p>", "proof": "<p>After removing artificial variables, Proposition 3 identifies the models with actual fixed-support cohomology, including derived central torsion. The global compact Selmer space is the line generated by nontorsion \\(P_K\\), with nonzero logarithm at each \\(p\\)-place. Proposition 4 gives rational strict acyclicity and\n\\[\nd(C_w^{s=1}(0),1)=-v_p(L_w^{s=1}(0)).\n\\]</p><p>Start from the all-Kummer volume\n\\[\nd(C_{\\mathrm{Kum}},(P_K,P_K^\\vee))\n=\n2n_P+2\\tau_g-s_K.\n\\]\nChoose a free local Kummer generator with logarithm generating \\(p^l\\mathbb Z_p\\). The point coordinate has valuation \\(v_p(\\log P_K)-l\\), and the local Kummer determinant in this rational basis has torsion volume \\(\\tau_p\\). Proposition 4’s strict–finite formula subtracts twice their sum.</p><p>Relaxing non-\\(p\\) Kummer conditions to full contributes \\(-\\tau_q\\) at each place: compact \\(H^1\\) is already Kummer and the quotient has degree-two torsion of that length. Each rational split \\(q\\) gives two places, proving the first formula.</p><p>Proposition 19 and Proposition 12’s exact center give\n\\[\nv_p(L_w^{s=1}(0))\n=\n-2v_p(c_E)+2v_p(\\log P_K)+2v_p(P_p(1))\n+2\\sum_{q\\in S^\\circ}v_p(P_q(1)).\n\\]\nEquate with the negative determinant volume and cancel the logarithms:\n\\[\n2n_P+2\\tau_g\n=\ns_K+2v_p(c_E)\n+2(\\tau_p-l-v_p(P_p(1)))\n+2\\sum_{q\\in S^\\circ}(\\tau_q-v_p(P_q(1))).\n\\]\nProposition 4’s Haar formulas identify the parentheses with the Tamagawa valuations. All bad primes are in support and omitted good components equal one.</p><p>Finally Proposition 2’s full-index identity, with\n\\[\nv_p([E(K):\\mathbb ZP_K])=n_P+\\tau_g,\n\\]\ngives \\(X_p(E)+X_p(E^D)=0\\). Its total real period, full regulator, and field-degree normalizations require no additional factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "Proposition 10.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every elliptic curve \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) of analytic rank zero or one and every odd prime \\(p\\), \\( v_p(Q_E)=v_p(\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)). \\)", "statement": "<p>For every elliptic curve \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) of analytic rank zero or one and every odd prime \\(p\\),\n\\[\nv_p(Q_E)=v_p(\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For non-CM \\(E\\), Proposition 11 gives \\(X_p(E)\\geq0\\). Its twist \\(E^D\\) is also non-CM and has complementary analytic rank, so \\(X_p(E^D)\\geq0\\). Proposition 20 makes their sum zero, hence \\(X_p(E)=0\\).</p><p>For CM rank zero, apply Lemma 21.1. For CM rank one, \\(E^D\\) is CM rank zero, and Lemma 21.1 together with Proposition 20 again gives \\(X_p(E)=0\\). The construction allowed every odd prime, including \\(p=3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.1", "orig": "Lemma 10.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) has complex multiplication and \\(L(E,1)\\neq0\\), then the exact BSD formula holds with Theorem 1’s normalizations.", "statement": "<p>If \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) has complex multiplication and \\(L(E,1)\\neq0\\), then the exact BSD formula holds with Theorem 1’s normalizations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(M\\) be the CM field. Burungale–Flach [5, Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 1] gives exact BSD for maximal-order CM curves \\(A/F\\) whose full torsion field over \\(M\\) is abelian and whose Hecke \\(L\\)-value at one is nonzero.</p><p>All endomorphisms of \\(E_M\\) are defined over \\(M\\). If its CM order is nonmaximal, replace each \\(T_\\ell E\\) by its \\(\\mathcal O_M\\otimes\\mathbb Z_\\ell\\)-span in \\(V_\\ell E\\). These Galois-stable lattices differ only at conductor primes and define an isogenous \\(A/M\\) with maximal-order CM. Its local CM Tate modules have rank one; commuting Galois actions make its full torsion extension abelian.</p><p>A nonzero integral trace-zero CM endomorphism changes sign under conjugation outside \\(M\\), giving a rational isogeny \\(E^M\\to E\\). Hence\n\\[\nL(E/M,s)=L(E,s)L(E^M,s)=L(E,s)^2.\n\\]\nNonvanishing at one gives the required nonzero Hecke factors. Apply Burungale–Flach and transfer exact BSD to \\(E_M\\) by isogeny.</p><p>Restriction of scalars satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Res}_{M/\\mathbb Q}E_M\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}E\\times E^M\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}E^2.\n\\]\nThus the normalized rational discrepancy has square one. Proposition 1 supplies positivity and rationality, so it equals one. This is the cited rational descent, with the lattice argument covering nonmaximal orders.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 24, "cite"], [1, 27, "cite"], [1, 43, "cite"], [1, 75, "cite"], [1, 77, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 38, "cite"], [8, 75, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 32, "cite"], [10, 37, "cite"], [10, 66, "cite"], [10, 75, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [14, 27, "cite"], [14, 37, "cite"], [14, 42, "cite"], [14, 49, "cite"], [14, 75, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 14, "cite"], [18, 14, "cite"], [19, 14, "cite"], [20, 36, "cite"], [20, 42, "cite"], [20, 49, "cite"], [20, 54, "cite"], [20, 69, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [22, 20, "cite"], [23, 20, "cite"], [24, 30, "cite"], [24, 31, "cite"], [24, 36, "cite"], [24, 49, "cite"], [24, 70, "cite"], [24, 71, "cite"], [25, 24, "part"], [26, 27, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [27, 24, "cite"], [28, 24, "cite"], [29, 24, "cite"], [30, 71, "cite"], [31, 30, "cite"], [32, 30, "cite"], [33, 36, "cite"], [33, 42, "cite"], [34, 33, "cite"], [35, 33, "cite"], [36, 42, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [38, 42, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [39, 41, "cite"], [40, 38, "cite"], [41, 38, "cite"], [42, 76, "cite"], [43, 48, "cite"], [43, 49, "cite"], [43, 50, "cite"], [43, 60, "cite"], [43, 70, "cite"], [43, 71, "cite"], [43, 75, "cite"], [44, 43, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [45, 43, "cite"], [46, 43, "cite"], [47, 43, "cite"], [48, 49, "cite"], [48, 70, "cite"], [49, 69, "cite"], [49, 74, "cite"], [50, 49, "cite"], [51, 49, "cite"], [52, 61, "cite"], [52, 62, "cite"], [52, 63, "cite"], [53, 54, "cite"], [53, 62, "cite"], [54, 52, "cite"], [54, 61, "cite"], [54, 62, "cite"], [55, 58, "cite"], [56, 52, "part"], [57, 58, "cite"], [57, 62, "cite"], [58, 52, "part"], [59, 60, "cite"], [60, 52, "cite"], [61, 70, "cite"], [61, 74, "cite"], [62, 61, "cite"], [62, 63, "cite"], [63, 61, "cite"], [64, 61, "cite"], [65, 61, "cite"], [66, 67, "cite"], [67, 61, "cite"], [67, 68, "cite"], [68, 61, "cite"], [69, 74, "cite"], [70, 69, "cite"], [71, 74, "cite"], [72, 73, "cite"], [73, 71, "cite"], [74, 75, "cite"], [75, 76, "cite"], [76, 0, "cite"], [77, 76, "cite"]]});
